window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2015-12-1", "source": "2015年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay based on the picture below. You should focus on the harm caused by misleading information online. You are required to write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "As is vividly depicted in the cartoon, a man sitting at his desk in front of a computer complains to a woman by\nthe door, \"I just feel unfortunate to live in a world with so much misleading information!\" Simple as it is, the\ndrawing conveys a thought-provoking message about how false and misleading content online has become an\ninescapable part of our daily lives.\nThe picture points to the alarming harm that misleading information does to modern society. To begin with,\nfalse content distorts people's judgment: readers who trust unverified rumors may make wrong decisions about\ntheir health, money or safety. Furthermore, when lies spread faster than facts, public trust in media and\ninstitutions is gradually undermined, leaving society more divided. Worse still, sensational fake news can\ntrigger needless panic, as groundless claims once drove crowds to panic-buy daily necessities.\nIn view of the above, it is high time that we took online misinformation seriously. Individuals should verify\ninformation before sharing it, and platforms ought to filter out false content responsibly. Only in this way can\nwe enjoy a cleaner and more trustworthy online world."}, "translation": {"prompt": "在中国,父母总是竭力帮助孩子，甚至为孩子做重要决定，而不管孩子想要什么,因为他们相信这样做是为孩子好。结果,孩子的成长和教育往往屈从于父母的意愿。 如果父母决定为孩子报名参加一个课外班,以增加其被重点学校录取的机会，他们会坚持自己的决定，即使孩子根本不感兴趣。 然而在美国,父母很可能会尊重孩子的意见，并在决策时更注重他们的意见。 中国父母十分重视教育或许值得称赞。然而，他们应向美国父母学习在涉及教育时如何平衡父母与子女间的关系。", "sample": "In China, parents always try their best to help their children and even make important decisions for them, regardless of what the children want, because they believe that doing so is good for the children. As a result, the growth and education of children often give way to their parents' wishes. If parents decide to sign their child up for an extracurricular class to increase the child's chances of being admitted to a top school, they will stick to their decision even if the child is not interested at all. In the United States, however, parents are likely to respect their children's opinions and pay more attention to them when making decisions. It may be commendable that Chinese parents attach great importance to education. However, they should learn from American parents how to balance the relationship between parents and children when it comes to education."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2015-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "According to a report from the Harvard School of Public Health, many everyday products, including some bug sprays and cleaning fluids, could lead to an increased risk of brain and behavioral disorders in children. The developing brain, the report says, is particularly  __26__  to the toxic effects of certain chemicals these products may contain, and the damage they cause can be  __27__  .\nThe official policy, however, is still evolving. Health and environmental  __28__  have long urged US government agencies to  __29__  the use of some of the 11 chemicals the report cites and called for more studies on their long-term effects. In 2001, for example, the Environmental Protection Agency  __30__  the type and amount of lead that could be present in paint and soil in homes and child-care  __31__  , after concerns were raised about lead poisoning. The agency is now  __32__  the toxic effects of some of the chemicals in the latest report.\nBut the threshold for regulation is high. Because children's brain and behavioral disorders, like hyperactivity and lower grades, can also be linked to social and genetic factors, it's tough to pin them on exposure to specific chemicals with solid  __33__  evidence, which is what the EPA requires. Even the Harvard study did not prove a direct  __34__  but noted strong associations between exposure and risk of behavioral issues.\nNonetheless, it's smart to  __35__  caution. While it may be impossible to prevent kids from drinking tap water that may contain trace amounts of chemicals, keeping kids away from lawns recently sprayed with chemicals and freshly dry-cleaned clothes can't hurt.", "matchIntro": "The Impossibility of Rapid Energy Transitions", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Politicians are fond of promising rapid energy transitions. Whether it is a transition from imported to domestic oil or from coal-powered electricity production to natural-gas power plants, politicians love to talk big. Unfortunately for them (and often the taxpayers), our energy systems are a bit like an aircraft carrier: they are unbelievably expensive, they are built to last for a very long time, they have a huge amount of inertia (meaning it takes a lot of energy to set them moving), and they have a lot of momentum once they are set in motion. No matter how hard you try, you can't turn something that large on a dime(10美分硬币), or even a few thousand dimes."}, {"label": "B", "text": "In physics, moving objects have two characteristics relevant to understanding the dynamics of energy systems: inertia and momentum. Inertia is the resistance of objects to efforts to change their state of motion. If you try to push a boulder(大圆石), it pushes you back. Once you have started the boulder rolling, it develops momentum, which is defined by its mass and velocity. Momentum is said to be \"conserved,\" that is, once you build it up, it has to go somewhere. So a heavy object, like a football player moving at a high speed, has a lot of momentum—that is, once he is moving, it is hard to change his state of motion. If you want to change his course, you have only a few choices: you can stop him, transferring (possibly painfully) some of his kinetic energy(动能) to your own body, or you can approach alongside and slowly apply pressure to gradually alter his course."}, {"label": "C", "text": "But there are other kinds of momentum as well. After all, we don't speak only of objects or people as having momentum; we speak of entire systems having momentum. Whether it's a sports team or a presidential campaign, everybody relishes having the big momentum, because it makes them harder to stop or change direction."}, {"label": "D", "text": "One kind of momentum is technological momentum. When a technology is deployed, its impacts reach far beyond itself. Consider the incandescent(白炽灯的) bulb, an object currently hated by many environmentalists and energy-efficiency advocates. The incandescent light bulb, invented by Thomas Edison, which came to be the symbol of inspiration, has been developed into hundreds, if not thousands, of forms. Today, a visit to a lighting store reveals a stunning array of choices. There are standard-shaped bulbs, flame-shaped bulbs, colored globe-shaped bulbs, and more. It is quite easy, with all that choice, to change a light bulb."}, {"label": "E", "text": "But the momentum of incandescent lighting does not stop there. All of those specialized bulbs led to the building of specialized light fixtures, from the desk lamp you study by, to the ugly but beloved hand-painted Chinese lamp you inherited from your grandmother, to the ceiling fixture in your closet, to the light in your oven or refrigerator, and to the light that the dentist points at you. It is easy to change a light bulb, sure, but it is harder to change the bulb and its fixture."}, {"label": "F", "text": "And there is more to the story, because not only are the devices that house incandescent bulbs shaped to their underlying characteristics, but rooms and entire buildings have been designed in accordance with how incandescent lighting reflects off walls and windows."}, {"label": "G", "text": "As lighting expert Howard Brandston points out, \"Generally, there are no bad light sources, only bad applications.\" There are some very commendable characteristics of the CFL [compact fluorescent(荧光的) light bulb], yet the selection of any light source remains inseparable from the luminaire(照明装置) that houses it, along with the space in which both are installed, and lighting requirements that need to be satisfied. The lamp, the fixture, and the room, all three must work in concert and for the true benefits of end-users. If the CFL should be used for lighting a particular space, or an object within that space, the fixture must be designed to work with that lamp, and that fixture with the room. It is a symbiotic(共生的) relationship. A CFL cannot be simply installed in an incandescent fixture and then expected to produce a visual appearance that is more than washed out, foggy, and dim. The whole fixture must be replaced—light source and luminaire—and this is never an inexpensive proposition."}, {"label": "H", "text": "And Brandston knows a thing or two about lighting, being the man who illuminated the Statue of Liberty."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Another type of momentum we have to think about when planning for changes in our energy systems is labor-pool momentum. It is one thing to say that we are going to shift 30 percent of our electricity supply from, say, coal to nuclear power in 20 years. But it is another thing to have a supply of trained talent that could let you carry out this promise. That is because the engineers, designers, regulators, operators, and all of the other skilled people needed for the new energy industry are specialists who have to be trained first (or retrained, if they are the ones being laid off in some related industry), and education, like any other complicated endeavor, takes time. And not only do our prospective new energy workers have to be trained, they have to be trained in the right sequence. One needs the designers, and perhaps the regulators, before the builders and operators, and each group of workers in training has to know there is work waiting beyond graduation. In some cases, colleges and universities might have to change their training programs, adding another layer of difficulty."}, {"label": "J", "text": "By far the biggest type of momentum that comes into play when it comes to changing our energy systems is economic momentum. The major components of our energy systems, such as fuel production, refining, electrical generation and distribution, are costly installations that have lengthy life spans. They have to operate for long periods of time before the costs of development have been recovered. When investors put up money to build, say, a nuclear power plant, they expect to earn that money back over the planned life of the plant, which is typically between 40 and 60 years. Some coal power plants in the United States have operated for more than 70 years! The oldest continuously operated commercial hydro-electric plant in the United States is on New York's Hudson River, and it went into commercial service in 1898."}, {"label": "K", "text": "As Vaclav Smil points out, \"All the forecasts, plans, and anticipations cited above have failed so miserably because their authors and promoters thought the transitions they hoped to implement would proceed unlike all previous energy transitions, and that their progress could be accelerated in an unprecedented manner.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "When you hear people speaking of making a rapid transition toward any type of energy, whether it is a switch from coal to nuclear power, or a switch from gasoline-powered cars to electric cars, or even a switch from an incandescent to a fluorescent light, understanding energy system inertia and momentum can help you decide whether their plans are feasible."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "One hundred years ago, \"Colored\" was the typical way of referring to Americans of African descent. Twenty years later, it was purposefully dropped to make way for \"Negro.\" By the late 1960s, that term was overtaken by \"Black.\" And then, at a press conference in Chicago in 1988, Jesse Jackson declared that \"African American\" was the term to embrace. This one was chosen because it echoed the labels of groups, such as \"Italian Americans\" and \"Irish Americans,\" that had already been freed of widespread discrimination.\nA century's worth of calculated name changes points to the fact that naming any group is a politically freighted exercise. A 2001 study cataloged all the ways in which the term \"Black\" carried connotations(涵义) that were more negative than those of \"African American.\"\nBut if it was known that \"Black\" people were viewed differently from \"African Americans,\" researchers, until now, hadn't identified what that gap in perception was derived from. A recent study, conducted by Emory University's Erika Hall, found that \"Black\" people are viewed more negatively than \"African Americans\" because of a perceived difference in socioeconomic status. As a result, \"Black\" people are thought of as less competent and as having colder personalities.\nThe study's most striking findings shed light on the racial biases permeating the professional world. Even seemingly harmless details on a resume, it appears, can tap into recruiters' biases. A job application might mention affiliations with groups such as the \"Wisconsin Association of African-American Lawyers\" or the \"National Black Employees Association,\" the names of which apparently have consequences, and are also beyond their members' control.\nIn one of the study's experiments, subjects were given a brief description of a man from Chicago with the last name Williams. To one group, he was identified as \"African-American,\" and another was told he was \"Black.\" With little else to go on, they were asked to estimate Mr. Williams's salary, professional standing, and educational background.\nThe \"African-American\" group estimated that he earned about $37,000 a year and had a two-year college degree. The \"Black\" group, on the other hand, put his salary at about $29,000, and guessed that he had only \"some\" college experience. Nearly three-quarters of the first group guessed that Mr. Williams worked at a managerial level, while only 38.5 percent of the second group thought so.\nHall's findings suggest there's an argument to be made for electing to use \"African American,\" though one can't help but get the sense that it's a decision that papers over the urgency of continued progress. Perhaps a new phrase is needed, one that can bring everyone one big step closer to realizing Du Bois's original, idealistic hope: \"It's not the name—it's the Thing that counts.\"", "47": "One hundred years ago, \"Colored\" was the typical way of referring to Americans of African descent. Twenty years later, it was purposefully dropped to make way for \"Negro.\" By the late 1960s, that term was overtaken by \"Black.\" And then, at a press conference in Chicago in 1988, Jesse Jackson declared that \"African American\" was the term to embrace. This one was chosen because it echoed the labels of groups, such as \"Italian Americans\" and \"Irish Americans,\" that had already been freed of widespread discrimination.\nA century's worth of calculated name changes points to the fact that naming any group is a politically freighted exercise. A 2001 study cataloged all the ways in which the term \"Black\" carried connotations(涵义) that were more negative than those of \"African American.\"\nBut if it was known that \"Black\" people were viewed differently from \"African Americans,\" researchers, until now, hadn't identified what that gap in perception was derived from. A recent study, conducted by Emory University's Erika Hall, found that \"Black\" people are viewed more negatively than \"African Americans\" because of a perceived difference in socioeconomic status. As a result, \"Black\" people are thought of as less competent and as having colder personalities.\nThe study's most striking findings shed light on the racial biases permeating the professional world. Even seemingly harmless details on a resume, it appears, can tap into recruiters' biases. A job application might mention affiliations with groups such as the \"Wisconsin Association of African-American Lawyers\" or the \"National Black Employees Association,\" the names of which apparently have consequences, and are also beyond their members' control.\nIn one of the study's experiments, subjects were given a brief description of a man from Chicago with the last name Williams. To one group, he was identified as \"African-American,\" and another was told he was \"Black.\" With little else to go on, they were asked to estimate Mr. Williams's salary, professional standing, and educational background.\nThe \"African-American\" group estimated that he earned about $37,000 a year and had a two-year college degree. The \"Black\" group, on the other hand, put his salary at about $29,000, and guessed that he had only \"some\" college experience. Nearly three-quarters of the first group guessed that Mr. Williams worked at a managerial level, while only 38.5 percent of the second group thought so.\nHall's findings suggest there's an argument to be made for electing to use \"African American,\" though one can't help but get the sense that it's a decision that papers over the urgency of continued progress. Perhaps a new phrase is needed, one that can bring everyone one big step closer to realizing Du Bois's original, idealistic hope: \"It's not the name—it's the Thing that counts.\"", "48": "One hundred years ago, \"Colored\" was the typical way of referring to Americans of African descent. Twenty years later, it was purposefully dropped to make way for \"Negro.\" By the late 1960s, that term was overtaken by \"Black.\" And then, at a press conference in Chicago in 1988, Jesse Jackson declared that \"African American\" was the term to embrace. This one was chosen because it echoed the labels of groups, such as \"Italian Americans\" and \"Irish Americans,\" that had already been freed of widespread discrimination.\nA century's worth of calculated name changes points to the fact that naming any group is a politically freighted exercise. A 2001 study cataloged all the ways in which the term \"Black\" carried connotations(涵义) that were more negative than those of \"African American.\"\nBut if it was known that \"Black\" people were viewed differently from \"African Americans,\" researchers, until now, hadn't identified what that gap in perception was derived from. A recent study, conducted by Emory University's Erika Hall, found that \"Black\" people are viewed more negatively than \"African Americans\" because of a perceived difference in socioeconomic status. As a result, \"Black\" people are thought of as less competent and as having colder personalities.\nThe study's most striking findings shed light on the racial biases permeating the professional world. Even seemingly harmless details on a resume, it appears, can tap into recruiters' biases. A job application might mention affiliations with groups such as the \"Wisconsin Association of African-American Lawyers\" or the \"National Black Employees Association,\" the names of which apparently have consequences, and are also beyond their members' control.\nIn one of the study's experiments, subjects were given a brief description of a man from Chicago with the last name Williams. To one group, he was identified as \"African-American,\" and another was told he was \"Black.\" With little else to go on, they were asked to estimate Mr. Williams's salary, professional standing, and educational background.\nThe \"African-American\" group estimated that he earned about $37,000 a year and had a two-year college degree. The \"Black\" group, on the other hand, put his salary at about $29,000, and guessed that he had only \"some\" college experience. Nearly three-quarters of the first group guessed that Mr. Williams worked at a managerial level, while only 38.5 percent of the second group thought so.\nHall's findings suggest there's an argument to be made for electing to use \"African American,\" though one can't help but get the sense that it's a decision that papers over the urgency of continued progress. Perhaps a new phrase is needed, one that can bring everyone one big step closer to realizing Du Bois's original, idealistic hope: \"It's not the name—it's the Thing that counts.\"", "49": "One hundred years ago, \"Colored\" was the typical way of referring to Americans of African descent. Twenty years later, it was purposefully dropped to make way for \"Negro.\" By the late 1960s, that term was overtaken by \"Black.\" And then, at a press conference in Chicago in 1988, Jesse Jackson declared that \"African American\" was the term to embrace. This one was chosen because it echoed the labels of groups, such as \"Italian Americans\" and \"Irish Americans,\" that had already been freed of widespread discrimination.\nA century's worth of calculated name changes points to the fact that naming any group is a politically freighted exercise. A 2001 study cataloged all the ways in which the term \"Black\" carried connotations(涵义) that were more negative than those of \"African American.\"\nBut if it was known that \"Black\" people were viewed differently from \"African Americans,\" researchers, until now, hadn't identified what that gap in perception was derived from. A recent study, conducted by Emory University's Erika Hall, found that \"Black\" people are viewed more negatively than \"African Americans\" because of a perceived difference in socioeconomic status. As a result, \"Black\" people are thought of as less competent and as having colder personalities.\nThe study's most striking findings shed light on the racial biases permeating the professional world. Even seemingly harmless details on a resume, it appears, can tap into recruiters' biases. A job application might mention affiliations with groups such as the \"Wisconsin Association of African-American Lawyers\" or the \"National Black Employees Association,\" the names of which apparently have consequences, and are also beyond their members' control.\nIn one of the study's experiments, subjects were given a brief description of a man from Chicago with the last name Williams. To one group, he was identified as \"African-American,\" and another was told he was \"Black.\" With little else to go on, they were asked to estimate Mr. Williams's salary, professional standing, and educational background.\nThe \"African-American\" group estimated that he earned about $37,000 a year and had a two-year college degree. The \"Black\" group, on the other hand, put his salary at about $29,000, and guessed that he had only \"some\" college experience. Nearly three-quarters of the first group guessed that Mr. Williams worked at a managerial level, while only 38.5 percent of the second group thought so.\nHall's findings suggest there's an argument to be made for electing to use \"African American,\" though one can't help but get the sense that it's a decision that papers over the urgency of continued progress. Perhaps a new phrase is needed, one that can bring everyone one big step closer to realizing Du Bois's original, idealistic hope: \"It's not the name—it's the Thing that counts.\"", "50": "One hundred years ago, \"Colored\" was the typical way of referring to Americans of African descent. Twenty years later, it was purposefully dropped to make way for \"Negro.\" By the late 1960s, that term was overtaken by \"Black.\" And then, at a press conference in Chicago in 1988, Jesse Jackson declared that \"African American\" was the term to embrace. This one was chosen because it echoed the labels of groups, such as \"Italian Americans\" and \"Irish Americans,\" that had already been freed of widespread discrimination.\nA century's worth of calculated name changes points to the fact that naming any group is a politically freighted exercise. A 2001 study cataloged all the ways in which the term \"Black\" carried connotations(涵义) that were more negative than those of \"African American.\"\nBut if it was known that \"Black\" people were viewed differently from \"African Americans,\" researchers, until now, hadn't identified what that gap in perception was derived from. A recent study, conducted by Emory University's Erika Hall, found that \"Black\" people are viewed more negatively than \"African Americans\" because of a perceived difference in socioeconomic status. As a result, \"Black\" people are thought of as less competent and as having colder personalities.\nThe study's most striking findings shed light on the racial biases permeating the professional world. Even seemingly harmless details on a resume, it appears, can tap into recruiters' biases. A job application might mention affiliations with groups such as the \"Wisconsin Association of African-American Lawyers\" or the \"National Black Employees Association,\" the names of which apparently have consequences, and are also beyond their members' control.\nIn one of the study's experiments, subjects were given a brief description of a man from Chicago with the last name Williams. To one group, he was identified as \"African-American,\" and another was told he was \"Black.\" With little else to go on, they were asked to estimate Mr. Williams's salary, professional standing, and educational background.\nThe \"African-American\" group estimated that he earned about $37,000 a year and had a two-year college degree. The \"Black\" group, on the other hand, put his salary at about $29,000, and guessed that he had only \"some\" college experience. Nearly three-quarters of the first group guessed that Mr. Williams worked at a managerial level, while only 38.5 percent of the second group thought so.\nHall's findings suggest there's an argument to be made for electing to use \"African American,\" though one can't help but get the sense that it's a decision that papers over the urgency of continued progress. Perhaps a new phrase is needed, one that can bring everyone one big step closer to realizing Du Bois's original, idealistic hope: \"It's not the name—it's the Thing that counts.\"", "51": "Across the board, American colleges and universities are not doing a very good job of preparing their students for the workplace or their post-graduation lives. This was made clear by the work of two sociologists, Richard Arum and Josipa Roksa. In 2011 they released a landmark study titled \"Academically Adrift,\" which documented the lack of intellectual growth experienced by many people enrolled in college. In particular, Arum and Roksa found, college students were not developing the critical thinking, analytic reasoning and other higher-level skills that are necessary to thrive in today's knowledge-based economy and to lead our nation in a time of complex challenges and dynamic change.\nArum and Roksa placed the blame for students' lack of learning on a watered-down college curriculum and lowered undergraduate work standards. Although going to college is supposed to be a full-time job, students spent, on average, only 12 to 14 hours a week studying and many were skating through their semesters without doing a significant amount of reading and writing. Students who take more challenging classes and spend more time studying do learn more. But the priorities of many undergraduates are with extracurricular activities, playing sports, and partying and socializing.\nLaura Hamilton, the author of a study on parents who pay for college, will argue in a forthcoming book that college administrations are overly concerned with the social and athletic activities of their students. In Paying for the Party, Hamilton describes what she calls the \"party pathway,\" which eases many students through college, helped along by various clubs that send students into the party scene and a host of easier majors. By sanctioning this watered-down version of college, universities are \"catering to the social and educational needs of wealthy students at the expense of others\" who won't enjoy the financial backing or social connections of richer students once they graduate.\nThese students need to build skills and knowledge during college if they are to use their degrees as a stepping-stone to middle-class mobility. But more privileged students must not waste this opportunity either. As recent graduates can testify, the job market isn't kind to candidates who can't demonstrate genuine competence, along with a well-cultivated willingness to work hard. Nor is the global economy forgiving of an American workforce with increasingly weak literacy, math and science abilities. College graduates will still fare better than those with only a high school education, of course. But a university degree unaccompanied by a gain in knowledge or skills is an empty achievement indeed. For students who have been coasting through college, and for American universities that have been demanding less work, offering more attractions and charging higher tuition, the party may soon be over.", "52": "Across the board, American colleges and universities are not doing a very good job of preparing their students for the workplace or their post-graduation lives. This was made clear by the work of two sociologists, Richard Arum and Josipa Roksa. In 2011 they released a landmark study titled \"Academically Adrift,\" which documented the lack of intellectual growth experienced by many people enrolled in college. In particular, Arum and Roksa found, college students were not developing the critical thinking, analytic reasoning and other higher-level skills that are necessary to thrive in today's knowledge-based economy and to lead our nation in a time of complex challenges and dynamic change.\nArum and Roksa placed the blame for students' lack of learning on a watered-down college curriculum and lowered undergraduate work standards. Although going to college is supposed to be a full-time job, students spent, on average, only 12 to 14 hours a week studying and many were skating through their semesters without doing a significant amount of reading and writing. Students who take more challenging classes and spend more time studying do learn more. But the priorities of many undergraduates are with extracurricular activities, playing sports, and partying and socializing.\nLaura Hamilton, the author of a study on parents who pay for college, will argue in a forthcoming book that college administrations are overly concerned with the social and athletic activities of their students. In Paying for the Party, Hamilton describes what she calls the \"party pathway,\" which eases many students through college, helped along by various clubs that send students into the party scene and a host of easier majors. By sanctioning this watered-down version of college, universities are \"catering to the social and educational needs of wealthy students at the expense of others\" who won't enjoy the financial backing or social connections of richer students once they graduate.\nThese students need to build skills and knowledge during college if they are to use their degrees as a stepping-stone to middle-class mobility. But more privileged students must not waste this opportunity either. As recent graduates can testify, the job market isn't kind to candidates who can't demonstrate genuine competence, along with a well-cultivated willingness to work hard. Nor is the global economy forgiving of an American workforce with increasingly weak literacy, math and science abilities. College graduates will still fare better than those with only a high school education, of course. But a university degree unaccompanied by a gain in knowledge or skills is an empty achievement indeed. For students who have been coasting through college, and for American universities that have been demanding less work, offering more attractions and charging higher tuition, the party may soon be over.", "53": "Across the board, American colleges and universities are not doing a very good job of preparing their students for the workplace or their post-graduation lives. This was made clear by the work of two sociologists, Richard Arum and Josipa Roksa. In 2011 they released a landmark study titled \"Academically Adrift,\" which documented the lack of intellectual growth experienced by many people enrolled in college. In particular, Arum and Roksa found, college students were not developing the critical thinking, analytic reasoning and other higher-level skills that are necessary to thrive in today's knowledge-based economy and to lead our nation in a time of complex challenges and dynamic change.\nArum and Roksa placed the blame for students' lack of learning on a watered-down college curriculum and lowered undergraduate work standards. Although going to college is supposed to be a full-time job, students spent, on average, only 12 to 14 hours a week studying and many were skating through their semesters without doing a significant amount of reading and writing. Students who take more challenging classes and spend more time studying do learn more. But the priorities of many undergraduates are with extracurricular activities, playing sports, and partying and socializing.\nLaura Hamilton, the author of a study on parents who pay for college, will argue in a forthcoming book that college administrations are overly concerned with the social and athletic activities of their students. In Paying for the Party, Hamilton describes what she calls the \"party pathway,\" which eases many students through college, helped along by various clubs that send students into the party scene and a host of easier majors. By sanctioning this watered-down version of college, universities are \"catering to the social and educational needs of wealthy students at the expense of others\" who won't enjoy the financial backing or social connections of richer students once they graduate.\nThese students need to build skills and knowledge during college if they are to use their degrees as a stepping-stone to middle-class mobility. But more privileged students must not waste this opportunity either. As recent graduates can testify, the job market isn't kind to candidates who can't demonstrate genuine competence, along with a well-cultivated willingness to work hard. Nor is the global economy forgiving of an American workforce with increasingly weak literacy, math and science abilities. College graduates will still fare better than those with only a high school education, of course. But a university degree unaccompanied by a gain in knowledge or skills is an empty achievement indeed. For students who have been coasting through college, and for American universities that have been demanding less work, offering more attractions and charging higher tuition, the party may soon be over.", "54": "Across the board, American colleges and universities are not doing a very good job of preparing their students for the workplace or their post-graduation lives. This was made clear by the work of two sociologists, Richard Arum and Josipa Roksa. In 2011 they released a landmark study titled \"Academically Adrift,\" which documented the lack of intellectual growth experienced by many people enrolled in college. In particular, Arum and Roksa found, college students were not developing the critical thinking, analytic reasoning and other higher-level skills that are necessary to thrive in today's knowledge-based economy and to lead our nation in a time of complex challenges and dynamic change.\nArum and Roksa placed the blame for students' lack of learning on a watered-down college curriculum and lowered undergraduate work standards. Although going to college is supposed to be a full-time job, students spent, on average, only 12 to 14 hours a week studying and many were skating through their semesters without doing a significant amount of reading and writing. Students who take more challenging classes and spend more time studying do learn more. But the priorities of many undergraduates are with extracurricular activities, playing sports, and partying and socializing.\nLaura Hamilton, the author of a study on parents who pay for college, will argue in a forthcoming book that college administrations are overly concerned with the social and athletic activities of their students. In Paying for the Party, Hamilton describes what she calls the \"party pathway,\" which eases many students through college, helped along by various clubs that send students into the party scene and a host of easier majors. By sanctioning this watered-down version of college, universities are \"catering to the social and educational needs of wealthy students at the expense of others\" who won't enjoy the financial backing or social connections of richer students once they graduate.\nThese students need to build skills and knowledge during college if they are to use their degrees as a stepping-stone to middle-class mobility. But more privileged students must not waste this opportunity either. As recent graduates can testify, the job market isn't kind to candidates who can't demonstrate genuine competence, along with a well-cultivated willingness to work hard. Nor is the global economy forgiving of an American workforce with increasingly weak literacy, math and science abilities. College graduates will still fare better than those with only a high school education, of course. But a university degree unaccompanied by a gain in knowledge or skills is an empty achievement indeed. For students who have been coasting through college, and for American universities that have been demanding less work, offering more attractions and charging higher tuition, the party may soon be over.", "55": "Across the board, American colleges and universities are not doing a very good job of preparing their students for the workplace or their post-graduation lives. This was made clear by the work of two sociologists, Richard Arum and Josipa Roksa. In 2011 they released a landmark study titled \"Academically Adrift,\" which documented the lack of intellectual growth experienced by many people enrolled in college. In particular, Arum and Roksa found, college students were not developing the critical thinking, analytic reasoning and other higher-level skills that are necessary to thrive in today's knowledge-based economy and to lead our nation in a time of complex challenges and dynamic change.\nArum and Roksa placed the blame for students' lack of learning on a watered-down college curriculum and lowered undergraduate work standards. Although going to college is supposed to be a full-time job, students spent, on average, only 12 to 14 hours a week studying and many were skating through their semesters without doing a significant amount of reading and writing. Students who take more challenging classes and spend more time studying do learn more. But the priorities of many undergraduates are with extracurricular activities, playing sports, and partying and socializing.\nLaura Hamilton, the author of a study on parents who pay for college, will argue in a forthcoming book that college administrations are overly concerned with the social and athletic activities of their students. In Paying for the Party, Hamilton describes what she calls the \"party pathway,\" which eases many students through college, helped along by various clubs that send students into the party scene and a host of easier majors. By sanctioning this watered-down version of college, universities are \"catering to the social and educational needs of wealthy students at the expense of others\" who won't enjoy the financial backing or social connections of richer students once they graduate.\nThese students need to build skills and knowledge during college if they are to use their degrees as a stepping-stone to middle-class mobility. But more privileged students must not waste this opportunity either. As recent graduates can testify, the job market isn't kind to candidates who can't demonstrate genuine competence, along with a well-cultivated willingness to work hard. Nor is the global economy forgiving of an American workforce with increasingly weak literacy, math and science abilities. College graduates will still fare better than those with only a high school education, of course. But a university degree unaccompanied by a gain in knowledge or skills is an empty achievement indeed. For students who have been coasting through college, and for American universities that have been demanding less work, offering more attractions and charging higher tuition, the party may soon be over."}, "questions": [{"id": "2015-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does Charles say songs can do when he sings them?", "options": ["A) Touch his heart.", "B) Make him cry.", "C) Remind him of his life.", "D) Make him feel young."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Touch his heart.\n【定位】No, not at this stage. I'm an old man. But the songs can still get through to me.\n不，现在这个阶段不会了。我老了。但歌still能打动我、touch到我的内心。\n【信号】转折 But 后才是真意。他先答 No, not at this stage.（现在唱歌不会让自己哭了），随后\n用 But the songs can still get through to me. 转折——But 之后才是他真正想表达的：\n歌依然能打动他。听力里转折词后面才是落点。\n【替换】the songs can still get through to me ↔ Touch his heart.\nget through to me = touch his heart\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 Make him cry. 抓的是录音里的 cry，但原文 No, not at this stage. 正是对\"唱歌会不\n会哭\"的否定回答——他明说现在不会哭了，B 取了被否定的内容。\n借⽤原⽂：No, not at this stage.\nC) 未提及\n选项 Remind him of his life. 说歌让他\"想起自己的人生\"，但他只说歌能 get through to\nme\"打动他\"，全程没说歌让他回忆人生，属凭空添加。\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 Make him feel young. 说歌让他\"感觉年轻\"，可原文相反——他强调 I'm an old\nman.\"我是个老人\"，并没说歌让他变年轻，方向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：I'm an old man.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does Charles say about himself as a singer?", "options": ["A) He is good at singing operas.", "B) He enjoys complicated music.", "C) He can sing any song if he likes it.", "D) He loves country music in particular."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He can sing any song if he likes it.\n【定位】If I like something, I can sing it. I did the opera Porgy and Bess, and that's said to be a\ncomplicated piece of music. And I can do country music, blues and love songs.\n如果我喜欢某样东西，我就能唱它。我演过歌剧《Porgy and Bess》，那被说成是一首很复\n杂的曲子。我也能唱乡村乐、布鲁斯和情歌。\n【信号】条件句 If I like… 是答案核。他回答\"什么最难唱\"时先抛出原则 If I like something, I can\nsing it.——这个\"只要喜欢就能唱\"的条件句是核心，后面举歌剧、乡村乐都是为它服务的\n例子。\n【替换】If I like something, I can sing it. ↔ He can sing any song if he likes it.\nlike something/can sing it=like it/can sing any song\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 He is good at singing operas. 抓住录音里的 opera，但原文 I did the opera Porgy\nand Bess 只说他\"演过\"一部歌剧，并没说他\"擅长唱歌剧\"，把\"做过\"夸大成\"擅长\"。\n借⽤原⽂：I did the opera Porgy and Bess\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 He enjoys complicated music. 抓的是 complicated，但原文 that's said to be a\ncomplicated piece of music 是\"别人说那首歌复杂\"，他本人没说\"喜欢复杂音乐\"，把客观\n评价安到了他的偏好上。\n借⽤原⽂：that's said to be a complicated piece of music\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 He loves country music in particular. 抓住 country music，但原文 I can do\ncountry music, blues and love songs. 把乡村乐和布鲁斯、情歌并列为\"我都能唱\"，并\n无\"尤其偏爱乡村乐\"之意，in particular 是凭空加的。\n借⽤原⽂：I can do country music, blues and love songs.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What's one of the man's weaknesses?", "options": ["A) He can't do such a complicated piece of music as the opera.", "B) He can't sing a song that he doesn't like.", "C) He can't concentrate on his singing sometimes.", "D) He can't play musical instrument while singing."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He can't sing a song that he doesn't like.\n【定位】On the other hand, I can't sing something I don't like and that's one of my defects.\n另一方面，我不能唱我不喜欢的东西，这是我的缺点之一。\n【信号】defect 自陈缺点+转折 On the other hand。他先说能唱各种喜欢的曲风，再用 On the\nother hand 转折，点出 I can't sing something I don't like and that's one of my\ndefects.——defect\"缺点\"是明确的答案信号词，转折后正是弱点所在。\n【替换】I can't sing something I don't like ↔ He can't sing a song that he doesn't like.\nsomething→a song，几乎逐字对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 He can't do such a complicated piece of music as the opera. 盗用\ncomplicated、opera，但原文 I did the opera Porgy and Bess 说他演过那部复杂歌剧、\n明明能做，A 说他\"做不了\"恰好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：I did the opera Porgy and Bess\nC) 未提及\n选项 He can't concentrate on his singing sometimes. 说他\"有时不能专注唱歌\"，原文从\n未提及专注问题，纯属编造。\nD) 未提及\n选项 He can't play musical instrument while singing. 说他\"不能边弹乐器边唱\"，原文通\n篇没谈乐器演奏，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What will Charles do when his personal life is hurting?", "options": ["A) Go to a bar and drink for hours.", "B) Go to an isolated place to sing blues.", "C) Go to see a performance in a concert hall.", "D) Go to work and wrap himself up in music."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Go to work and wrap himself up in music.\n【定位】I'm the kind of person that if my personal life is hurting, I can go to work and the music will\ntake over.\n我是这样一种人：如果我的个人生活在受煎熬，我可以去工作，让音乐接管一切（把我整个\n裹进去）。\n【信号】条件 if my personal life is hurting 后接应对。他说 if my personal life is hurting, I can\ngo to work and the music will take over——条件\"个人生活受伤\"与题干的 when 对应，\n其后的 go to work、music will take over 就是他的应对动作。\n【替换】I can go to work and the music will take over ↔ Go to work and wrap himself up in music.\ngo to work 原词+music take over=wrap up in music\nI can wrap myself up in my music ↔ Go to work and wrap himself up in music.\nwrap myself up=wrap himself up，仅人称变\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Go to a bar and drink for hours. 抓住 bar、drink，但原文 It's like a guy who\ngoes to a bar and drinks. 是个比喻——他说自己沉浸音乐\"就像别人去酒吧喝酒\"，喝酒的\n是\"别人/那个比喻里的人\"，不是他真要做的。\n借⽤原⽂：It's like a guy who goes to a bar and drinks.\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 Go to an isolated place to sing blues. 借用前文出现过的 blues（I can do country\nmusic, blues and love songs.），但那是他列举能唱的曲风，与\"难过时怎么办\"无关，且\n原文没说\"去僻静处唱布鲁斯\"。\n借⽤原⽂：I can do country music, blues and love songs.\nC) 未提及\n选项 Go to see a performance in a concert hall. 说他去音乐厅看演出，原文只说他难过\n时\"去工作、投入自己的音乐\"，从未提到去音乐厅看别人表演，属编造。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the woman want to know about the man?", "options": ["A) How he became an announcer.", "B) How he writes news stories.", "C) How he makes his living.", "D) How he does his job."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） How he does his job.\n【定位】W: I wonder if you could tell me a little bit about your job as a radio announcer. What do you\nactually do and how do you prepare for your job?\n女：不知道您能否给我讲讲您作为电台播音员这份工作？您实际上做些什么、又是怎么为这\n份工作做准备的？\n【信号】开场提问句即主线。答案信号是『女士的开场提问』。她一上来就说 tell me a little bit\nabout your job as a radio announcer，并追问 What do you actually do——这正是访\n谈的总问题，定位锚就是开头女士抛出的那一问。\n【替换】What do you actually do ↔ How he does his job.\ndo what→does his job 同义概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 How he became an announcer.（他如何成为播音员）。录音里确实出现 radio\nannouncer 这个词，听到 announcer 容易顺手选 A；但女士问的是『你做什么、怎么准\n备』，从没问他『怎么入行』。这是典型原词陷阱：盗用 announcer 一词，却把『做这份\n工作』偷换成『成为播音员』。\n借⽤原⽂：radio announcer\nB) 答非所问\nB 说 How he writes news stories.（他如何写新闻稿）。news stories 在后文反复出现，\n容易误当本题答案；但『写稿』是后面 news subs 那一题（第6题）的内容，并非女士开场\n想了解的总主题。本题问的是整体工作，B 是把细节误当主旨。\n借⽤原⽂：write the news stories\nC) 未提及\nC 说 How he makes his living.（他如何谋生）。整段对话只谈工作内容与流程，从没涉及\n收入、谋生话题。makes his living 与 How he does his job. 字面相近（都含『工作』\n味）易被误选，实则属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say news subs do?", "options": ["A) They write the first version of news stories.", "B) They gather news stories on the spot.", "C) They polish incoming news stories.", "D) They write comments on major news stories."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They write the first version of news stories.\n【定位】M: They are sub-editors. They are the people who write the news stories as they come in.\n男：他们是助理编辑。他们是新闻一进来就负责把它写成稿的人。\n【信号】被问者对名词的直接定义句。答案信号是『被问者对该角色的直接定义应答』。女士追问\nnews subs 是什么，男士直接答 They are the people who write the news stories as\nthey come in——这是对所问对象的下定义句，正是承载答案的那一句。\n【替换】write the news stories as they come in ↔ They write the first version of news stories.\nas they come in→the first version 同义\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 说 They gather news stories on the spot.（现场采集新闻）。录音确有 news stories，\n听到就想选；但男士说他们是 write the news stories as they come in——是『写』不是\n『采集现场』，news subs 坐在编辑室里，并不外出跑现场。盗用 news stories 一词，却\n把『写稿』偷换成『现场采集』。\n借⽤原⽂：write the news stories as they come in\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 说 They polish incoming news stories.（润色进来的新闻稿）。录音后文确有 they are\nrefined, corrected and sorted out（被润色、订正、整理），听到 refined 易联想 polish\n选 C；但那是稿子往下传给 senior duty editor、assistant editor 之后才发生的，不是\nnews subs 干的。这是张冠李戴：把后续编辑的活儿安到 news subs 身上。\n借⽤原⽂：they are refined, corrected and sorted out\nD) 未提及\nD 说 They write comments on major news stories.（就重大新闻写评论）。录音只说\nnews subs『写稿』，全程没提『写评论』。借 write 与 news stories 的原词凑出一个似是\n而非的职责，属无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：write the news stories", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the man say is a big challenge for him?", "options": ["A) Reading through the news stories in a given period of time.", "B) Having little time to read the news before going on the air.", "C) Having to change the tone of his voice from time to time.", "D) Getting all the words and phrases pronounced correctly."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Having little time to read the news before going on the air.\n【定位】Of course, sometimes things happen at the last moment and I don't have a chance. So I've just\ngot to do my best, and take a couple of seconds to look through the first few lines before I\nlaunch into something.\n当然，有时事情在最后一刻才发生，我就没机会（事先通读）了。所以我只能尽力而为，在\n开口播之前抢出几秒钟扫一眼开头几行。\n【信号】转折 of course/sometimes 引出难处。答案信号是转折让步语气。男士先说一般能 read\nthrough most stories before I go on the air（上节目前能通读多数稿），随即用 Of\ncourse, sometimes things happen at the last moment and I don't have a chance 转出\n难处——『有时来不及在上节目前读稿』，这正是承载挑战的那句。\n【替换】sometimes things happen at the last moment and I don't have a chance ↔\nHaving little time to read the news before going on the air.\ndon't have a chance→little time 同义\nbefore I go on the air ↔ before going on the air\ngo on the air=going on the air 同指\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 Reading through the news stories in a given period of time.（在规定时间内读完\n新闻稿）。录音有 read through most stories 字样，听到 read through 易选 A；但录音\n说的是『有时没机会读』，并非『限定时段内读完』，in a given period of time 是凭空加\n的。盗用 read through，却篡改成另一种难处。\n借⽤原⽂：read through most stories\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说 Having to change the tone of his voice from time to time.（不得不时常变换语\n气）。录音确有 if you start off in a bright tone 提到 tone，听到就想选；但那是说万一\n一开口语气欢快却发现在播车祸死人的尴尬，并非把『变换语气』当成挑战。盗用 tone 一\n词，编出原文没有的难处。\n借⽤原⽂：if you start off in a bright tone\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 Getting all the words and phrases pronounced correctly.（让所有词句发音正\n确）。录音末尾确有 checking pronunciations，听到 pronunciations 易选 D；但男士只\n是顺带说工作时『核对发音』，从未把它说成 big challenge。盗用 pronunciations，过度\n拔高成挑战。\n借⽤原⽂：checking pronunciations", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What role does the man say his small cross plays?", "options": ["A) It shows where advertisements come in.", "B) It gives a signal for him to slow down.", "C) It alerts him to something important.", "D) It serves as a reminder of sad news."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It serves as a reminder of sad news.\n【定位】No, I have my own little mark. If it's something sad, I put a small cross at the top. That's my\nlittle clue.\n没有，我有我自己的小记号。如果是悲伤的内容，我就在顶端画一个小叉。那就是我的小\n提示。\n【信号】条件句 If it's something sad 点明用途。答案信号是条件句给出的使用场景。男士说 If\nit's something sad, I put a small cross at the top——这个小叉是专为『悲伤内容』而\n画，用途锁定在 sad 上，正是答案句。\n【替换】If it's something sad, I put a small cross ↔ It serves as a reminder of sad news.\n为 sad 而画→sad news 的提醒\nThat's my little clue ↔ reminder\nclue→reminder 同义\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说 It shows where advertisements come in.（标示广告插入处）。整段从没提广告，\n这是凭空想象电台与广告的关联，属无中生有，与小叉的真实用途无关。\nB) 偷换概念\nB 说 It gives a signal for him to slow down.（提示他放慢）。录音说小叉是 my little\nclue（提示）没错，但提示的是『这是悲伤内容』，绝非『放慢语速』。借 clue 的『提\n示』之意，偷换成另一种用途。\n借⽤原⽂：That's my little clue\nC) 偷换概念\nC 说 It alerts him to something important.（提醒他注意要事）。这是本题最像答案的陷\n阱：录音说小叉提醒 something sad（悲伤的事），C 偷偷把范围放宽成 something\nimportant（重要的事）。悲伤的不一定要标成『重要』，男士说得很死——只标 sad，C\n范围偷改，排除。\n借⽤原⽂：If it's something sad", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the speaker say about America's universities?", "options": ["A) They offer students a wide variety of courses.", "B) They attract students from all over the world.", "C) They admit more students than they can handle.", "D) They have trouble dealing with overseas students."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They attract students from all over the world.\n【定位】For all their troubles, the United States' 3,500 institutions were flooded with more than half a\nmillion students from 193 countries last year.\n尽管问题重重,去年美国这 3500 所院校仍涌入了来自 193 个国家的逾五十万名学生。\n【信号】开篇主题句+量化佐证。这句紧接首句“美国大学是世界艳羡的对象”之后,用 more than half\na million students from 193 countries 这组数字坐实“吸引全球学生”,是说话人对美国大学\n下的核心论断,故为答案句。\n【替换】flooded with more than half a million students from 193 countries ↔\nattract students from all over the world\n“193 个国家涌入”=“吸引全世界学生”\n【排除】A) 未提及\nThey offer students a wide variety of courses. 说美国大学课程门类多,但录音通篇讲的是\n生源数量与教育理念,从没说过课程多寡,属无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey admit more students than they can handle. 借录音里“学生多”(more than half a\nmillion students)的印象,扭成“招的学生超出处理能力”这一负面意思;录音夸的是“吸引力强”,\n从没说“应付不了”,听到“学生多”就选它正中陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：more than half a million students from 193 countries\nD) 与原文相反\nThey have trouble dealing with overseas students. 借录音里反复出现的“外国学生”\n(students from 193 countries、foreigners who have passed their high school exams)\n做诱饵,却说成“应付外国学生有困难”;录音说的是美国大学对外国学生 far more\nwelcoming(更欢迎),态度恰好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：find American universities far more welcoming", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What is Americans' view on education according to Prof. Marvin Bresler?", "options": ["A) Everyone will benefit from education sooner or later.", "B) A good education contributes to the prosperity of a nation.", "C) A good education is necessary for one to climb the social ladder.", "D) Everyone has a right to an education appropriate to his potential."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Everyone has a right to an education appropriate to his potential.\n【定位】The conception that everyone has a right to an education appropriate to his potential is\na highly democratic and compassionate standard, says Marvin Bresler, a professor at\nPrinceton University.\n普林斯顿大学教授马文·布雷斯勒说,“人人都有权接受与其潜能相称的教育”这一理念,是一条\n极具民主与同情心的准则。\n【信号】人名 says 引述标志句。句尾的 says Marvin Bresler 是明确的引述信号,把前面那句教育理\n念归到 Bresler 名下;题干问的就是他的 view,这句正是答案句。\n【替换】everyone has a right to an education appropriate to his potential ↔\nEveryone has a right to an education appropriate to his potential.\n理念句几乎原句对应\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nEveryone will benefit from education sooner or later. 借了 everyone 这个词显得像 D,\n但录音 Bresler 说的是“人人有权受按潜能的教育”,讲的是“权利”不是“迟早受益”,sooner or\nlater 录音从未提及。\n借⽤原⽂：everyone has a right to an education appropriate to his potential\nB) 未提及\nA good education contributes to the prosperity of a nation. 把教育和“国家繁荣”挂钩,\n这是听起来很对的大道理,但录音 Bresler 谈的是个人按潜能受教育的权利,完全没扯到国家繁\n荣,属无中生有。\nC) 未提及\nA good education is necessary for one to climb the social ladder. 讲教育是个人晋升的\n必需品,听着也合理,但 Bresler 强调的是“按潜能受教育的权利”,从没提“社会阶梯”或“向上爬”,\n属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What do we learn from Prof. Christopher Ricks' remark?", "options": ["A) He likes students with high motivation.", "B) He enjoys teaching intelligent students.", "C) He tailors his teaching to students' needs.", "D) He treats all his students in a fair manner."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） He likes students with high motivation.\n【定位】\"I'm not against elitism, but I happen to like having people who are more eager to learn,\" says\nProf. Christopher Ricks at Boston University.\n波士顿大学的克里斯托弗·里克斯教授说:“我并不反对精英教育,只是我恰好喜欢那些更渴望学\n习的人。”\n【信号】转折 but 后的第一人称表态。这句的真意在转折词 but 之后:I happen to like having\npeople who are more eager to learn 是 Ricks 用“I like”直接亮出的偏好,转折后+第一人\n称两个信号叠加,锁定这就是答案句。\n【替换】people who are more eager to learn ↔ students with high motivation\n“更渴望学习”=“高学习动机”\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nHe enjoys teaching intelligent students. 借录音里真念到的 as intelligent as the much\nnarrower band 这个 intelligent 做诱饵,但 Ricks 是拿“聪明”作对照,转折后明说自己偏爱的\n是“爱学”而非“聪明”,听到 intelligent 就选它正中陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：as intelligent as the much narrower band in British universities\nC) 未提及\nHe tailors his teaching to students' needs. 讲 Ricks 按学生需求调整教学,这是听着很正\n面的教学法,但 Ricks 只说了自己喜欢哪种学生,从没提怎么“调整教学”,属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nHe treats all his students in a fair manner. 说 Ricks 对所有学生一视同仁,但他恰恰明说\n“更喜欢爱学的人”(I happen to like having people who are more eager to learn),这本身\n就是有偏好,与“一视同仁”矛盾。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the speaker say about energy used in the United States?", "options": ["A) It is mostly imported from the Middle East.", "B) It is a sure indicator of its economic activity.", "C) It has a direct impact on the international oil market.", "D) It equals more than 30 million barrels of oil each day."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It equals more than 30 million barrels of oil each day.\n【定位】Every year, people in America use energy equal to over 30 million barrels of oil each day.\n每年，美国人使用的能源相当于每天三千多万桶石油。\n【信号】开篇首句直陈数字。录音第一句 Every year, people in America use energy equal to\nover 30 million barrels of oil each day. 就以数字直陈美国能源用量，篇章独白常把核心\n事实放在开篇，听到 30 million barrels 这个数字就该锁定它是答案句。\n【替换】energy equal to over 30 million barrels of oil each day ↔\nIt equals more than 30 million barrels of oil each day.\nover 与 more than 同义、数字单位照搬\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt is mostly imported from the Middle East. 说能源主要从中东进口，但录音通篇只讲用\n量与效率，从未提进口或中东，是未提及的编造。\nB) 过度推断\nIt is a sure indicator of its economic activity. 把能源用量说成经济活动的可靠指标，这是\n一步推断，录音只客观报了用量，没说它能指示经济，属过度推断。\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt has a direct impact on the international oil market. 借 oil 这个录音里确实出现的词，\n引出对国际油市的直接影响，但录音从未提油市或影响，是听到 oil 就想选的原词诱惑。\n借⽤原⽂：30 million barrels of oil", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What do scientists say about energy contained in fuel?", "options": ["A) It eventually turns into heat.", "B) It is used in a variety of forms.", "C) Its use is chiefly responsible for air pollution.", "D) Part of it is lost in the process of transmission."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It eventually turns into heat.\n【定位】Viewed from a scientist's standpoint, all of the energy contained in fuel, either now or in the\nfuture, becomes heat.\n从科学家的角度看，燃料中所含的全部能量，无论是现在还是将来，最终都会变成热。\n【信号】标志短语 from a scientist's standpoint 后接结论。录音的 Viewed from a scientist's\nstandpoint, all of the energy contained in fuel, either now or in the future, becomes\nheat. 以 from a scientist's standpoint 点明这正是科学家的观点，紧跟其后的 becomes\nheat 就是答案落点，听到这个标志短语要立刻竖起耳朵。\n【替换】all of the energy contained in fuel, either now or in the future, becomes heat ↔\nIt eventually turns into heat.\nbecomes heat 改写为 turns into heat、either now or in future 概括成 eventually\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nIt is used in a variety of forms. 里的 forms 借自录音 That's energy in all forms，但那句\n讲的是能源以多种形式存在，不是科学家对燃料中能量的结论，张冠李戴到了本题。\n借⽤原⽂：That's energy in all forms\nC) 未提及\nIts use is chiefly responsible for air pollution. 说能量使用是空气污染主因，录音谈的是\n能量变热与效率，从未提空气污染，是谈能源时易顺手补上的常识联想。\nD) 原词陷阱\nPart of it is lost in the process of transmission. 借了录音后文 The rest is lost or\nrejected 的 lost，但录音说的是部分热量散失到大气，不是传输过程损失，且这也不是科学\n家那句的结论，听到 lost 就想选会落坑。\n借⽤原⽂：The rest is lost or rejected, radiated into the atmosphere", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "When does mass transit prove superior to private vehicles?", "options": ["A) When it is used in rural areas.", "B) When it is environment-friendly.", "C) When it operates at near capacity.", "D) When it operates at regular times."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） When it operates at near capacity.\n【定位】In terms of efficiency and cleanness, buses, trains and other forms of public or mass\ntransportation are superior to the private automobile, but only if they operate at near capacity.\n就效率和清洁而言，公共汽车、火车等公共或大众交通工具优于私家车，但前提是它们要接\n近满载运行。\n【信号】限定词 but only if 后才是条件。录音的 are superior to the private automobile, but\nonly if they operate at near capacity 在 but only if 之后才给出真正的成立条件，转折加\n限定词后面的小句才是答案落点，前半的笼统说优越是铺垫。\n【替换】only if they operate at near capacity ↔ When it operates at near capacity.\nonly if 转写为 When、operate at near capacity 原义照搬\n【排除】A) 未提及\nWhen it is used in rural areas. 说在乡村使用时更优，录音给的条件是接近满载，从未提\n乡村，是未提及的编造条件。\nB) 原词陷阱\nWhen it is environment-friendly. 把录音 In terms of efficiency and cleanness 里的\ncleanness 联想成环保，但那是评判优越的角度，不是优越成立的条件，听到清洁就想选会\n偏。\n借⽤原⽂：In terms of efficiency and cleanness\nD) 偷换概念\nWhen it operates at regular times. 与正确项只差末尾，把录音的 near capacity（接近满\n载）偷换成 regular times（定时运行），录音从未说定时，是仅改关键词的近似干扰。\n借⽤原⽂：only if they operate at near capacity", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What seems to be the speaker's biggest concern?", "options": ["A) Traffic jams in cities.", "B) Inefficient use of energy.", "C) Fuel shortage.", "D) Global warming."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） Inefficient use of energy.\n【定位】If we could figure out how to improve the efficiency with which we use energy, we could do\na lot more work with the energy that is available. ... about three quarters of the energy we use\nto move things, including ourselves, accomplishes no useful work.\n如果我们能想出办法提高用能的效率，就能用现有的能源做更多的事。……我们用来移动物\n体（包括我们自己）的能源，约有四分之三没有完成任何有用的功。\n【信号】efficiency 反复出现、四分之三能源做无用功。录音多处回到效率：improve the\nefficiency with which we use energy、boost efficiency，并以 about three quarters of\nthe energy we use to move things … accomplishes no useful work 点出大量能源被浪\n费，这条贯穿全篇的浪费主线就是关切所在。\n【替换】about three quarters of the energy we use to move things, including ourselves, accomplishes no\nuseful work\n↔ Inefficient use of energy.\n四分之三能源做无用功概括为能源使用低效\n【排除】A) 未提及\nTraffic jams in cities. 借录音谈 transportation 的语境联想出堵车，但录音讲交通是为说明\n能效，从未提堵车，是顺着交通话题想出来的未提及项。\nC) 偷换概念\nFuel shortage. 借录音里 fuel、lost 等词联想到燃料短缺，但录音担心的是能源用得浪费、\n效率低，不是没有燃料可用，把低效偷换成短缺。\n借⽤原⽂：The rest is lost or rejected\nD) 未提及\nGlobal warming. 是讨论能源时人人会联想的热词，但录音通篇没出现 global warming 或\n气候字眼，纯属凭印象误选，录音未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "Which of the following is a mood-boosting habit according to Rubin?", "options": ["A) Smelling scented candles.", "B) Setting an alarm for waking up.", "C) Talking with your parents often.", "D) Listening to an upsetting song."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Smelling scented candles.\n【定位】I used to laugh at scented candles but now I understand scents are a way to get a quick lift\nwithout any time, energy and money.\n我过去常嘲笑香薰蜡烛，但现在我明白，香味是一种不花任何时间、精力和金钱就能让心情\n快速提振的方式。\n【信号】转折 but 之后＋点出功效。这句用 but now I understand 把态度从“嘲笑”扭转为“认可”，\n转折后才是讲者的真意，而 a way to get a quick lift（快速提振心情）正好对上题干要的\nmood-boosting habit，所以这句是答案句。\n【替换】scented candles ↔ Smelling scented candles\n原文物件＝选项动作对象\na way to get a quick lift ↔ Smelling scented candles\n“快速提振”＝题干要的 mood-boosting\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n原文确实念到了 SET AN ALARM-AND NOT JUST FOR WAKING UP，B 项 Setting an\nalarm for waking up. 借了 alarm 这个原词，却恰好取了原文明确否定的“为起床而定闹钟”\n——讲者要的是睡前闹钟，方向被改反。\n借⽤原⽂：SET AN ALARM-AND NOT JUST FOR WAKING UP\nC) 偷换概念\nC 项 Talking with your parents often. 听着像原文谈“关系让人快乐”那段，但原文\nrelationships are a key 说的是亲吻所爱、加深各种关系，从未具体提“常和父母聊天”，是\n把泛泛的“关系”偷换成了具体的“和父母聊天”。\n借⽤原⽂：relationships are a key\nD) 与原文相反\n原文标题是 LISTEN TO AN UPBEAT SONG（听欢快的歌），D 项 Listening to an\nupsetting song. 借了“听歌”原词，却把 upbeat（欢快）换成形近又反义的 upsetting（令\n人沮丧），令人沮丧的歌显然不能提振心情。\n借⽤原⽂：LISTEN TO AN UPBEAT SONG", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What is \"ONE-MINUTE RULE\" according to the lecturer?", "options": ["A) Handle the overwhelming unopened mail and tiny tasks within one minute.", "B) Do anything that can be done in less than one minute without delay.", "C) Do everything that can't be done in a minute within one minute.", "D) Documents must be printed out and filed within one minute."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Do anything that can be done in less than one minute without delay.\n【定位】The one-minute rule says that anything I can do in less than a minute, I should do\nwithout delay.\n“一分钟法则”是说：任何我能在一分钟内做完的事，我都应当立刻去做、不拖延。\n【信号】定义句标志 says that。这句以 The one-minute rule says that 直接给规则下定义，says\nthat 后面的整句就是该术语的官方解释，是不折不扣的答案句。\n【替换】anything I can do in less than a minute ↔ anything that can be done in less than one minute\n范围词 anything 与时限完全保留\nI should do without delay ↔ without delay\n“立刻做、不拖延”原义照搬\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项 Handle the overwhelming unopened mail and tiny tasks within one minute. 盗\n用了原文 Overwhelmed by unopened mail, and other tiny tasks 这句引入语，却把适用\n于“任何一分钟内能做之事”的规则缩成只管“未拆信件和琐事”——那只是举例的背景，不是\n规则本身。\n借⽤原⽂：Overwhelmed by unopened mail, and other tiny tasks\nC) 与原文相反\nC 项 Do everything that can't be done in a minute within one minute. 借了“一分钟”框\n架，却把原文的 anything I can do in less than a minute 反成“做不完的也要一分钟内做\n完”，既反了原义又自相矛盾。\n借⽤原⽂：anything I can do in less than a minute\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文 printing out a document and filing it 只是“一分钟内能做之事”的众多举例之一，D\n项 Documents must be printed out and filed within one minute. 盗用这个例子，用\nmust 把它说成必须执行的硬规定，以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：printing out a document and filing it", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does \"BRIGHT-LINE RULES\" refer to?", "options": ["A) Strict habits.", "B) Positive attitude.", "C) Hard rules.", "D) Decision-making methods."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Hard rules.\n【定位】I believe habits have a direct connection with happiness, and I think hard rules can spare one\nthe agony of decision-making.\n我相信习惯与幸福有直接联系，而我认为硬性规则能让人免去做决定的痛苦。\n【信号】术语紧跟其后的同位定性词 hard rules。标题 MAKE SOME \"BRIGHT-LINE RULES\" 之\n后，讲者用 hard rules can spare one the agony of decision-making 来解释它，hard\nrules 正是给这个术语定性的核心名词，故这句是答案句。\n【替换】hard rules ↔ Hard rules\n原文定性词与选项逐字相同\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文确实念到 habits have a direct connection with happiness，A 项 Strict habits. 借了\nhabits 这个原词，但讲者说的是习惯与幸福有关，并未把 BRIGHT-LINE RULES 定性为“严\n格的习惯”，且 strict 也是原文没有的。\n借⽤原⽂：habits have a direct connection with happiness\nB) 未提及\nB 项 Positive attitude. 在讲 BRIGHT-LINE RULES 的段落里没有任何依据，讲者谈的是习\n惯、硬规则与决策，从未把它说成一种“积极态度”，属凭空添加。\nD) 答非所问\n原文 hard rules can spare one the agony of decision-making 说硬规则能省去做决定的\n痛苦，D 项 Decision-making methods. 盗用 decision-making 一词，却把术语错当成\n“决策方法”——而原文里决策恰是被规则“省去”的对象，不是规则的所指。\n借⽤原⽂：hard rules can spare one the agony of decision-making", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does \"a person can never be too rich\" mean according to the talk?", "options": ["A) It takes great pains for a person to be very rich.", "B) Not Everyone wants to be too rich.", "C) A person will never be satisfied with his wealth.", "D) A person will never have the feeling of being wealthy."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） A person will never be satisfied with his wealth.\n【定位】Almost every language in the world has a saying that a person can never be too rich.\nAmericans, like people in other countries, always want more money.\n世界上几乎每种语言都有一句话：一个人永远不会嫌自己太富。美国人和其他国家的人一\n样，总是想要更多的钱。\n【信号】紧随其后的解释句。判定信号是\"说法 + 紧接着的解释\"结构：录音先抛出 a person can\nnever be too rich，下一句立刻用 always want more money 解释它的真实含义，这句解\n释就是答案落点。\n【替换】always want more money ↔ never be satisfied with his wealth\n肯否定转换的同义改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt takes great pains for a person to be very rich 说\"变富要费很大力气\"，录音通篇讲的\n是\"人总嫌钱不够\"，从未谈\"致富艰辛\"，属未提及。\nB) 与原文相反\nNot Everyone wants to be too rich 说\"不是人人都想太富\"，恰与录音 always want\nmore money 及\"几乎每种语言都有这句话\"的普遍性相反，方向反了。\n借⽤原⽂：always want more money\nD) 偷换概念\nA person will never have the feeling of being wealthy 把\"客观上总想要更多钱\"偷换\n成\"主观上从无富有的感觉\"，录音说的是欲望（想要更多），不是说人永远感觉不到自己\n富，概念被掉包。\n借⽤原⽂：always want more money", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What's a person's purpose of saying \"I am working for chickenfeed\"?", "options": ["A) Introducing a topic.", "B) Telling a joke.", "C) Making a complaint.", "D) Making a decision."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） Making a complaint.\n【定位】One way they express this is by protesting that their jobs do not pay enough. A common\nexpression is, \"I am working for chickenfeed.\"\n他们表达这一点的一种方式，就是不满地抱怨自己的工作报酬不够。一个常见的说法\n是：\"我在为鸡饲料般的钱工作。\"\n【信号】动词 protesting 点明语气。答案信号是动词 protesting（不满地抱怨）：录音明说人\n们\"by protesting that their jobs do not pay enough\"——是在抱怨工资低，这正是说\nchickenfeed 这句话的目的。\n【替换】protesting that their jobs do not pay enough ↔ Making a complaint\nprotest 与 complaint 同义\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nIntroducing a topic 看似贴切（录音开头确在引入话题），但本题问的是说\"I am working\nfor chickenfeed\"这一句的目的，这句的目的是抱怨工资低，不是引入话题；混淆了\"整段开\n场\"与\"这一句\"的目的。\nB) 未提及\nTelling a joke 录音里没有任何\"开玩笑\"的语气标记，说这句话时是 protesting（抱怨），\n并非玩笑，属未提及。\nD) 未提及\nMaking a decision 录音中说话人只是抱怨工资低，并未做出任何决定（如辞职、换工\n作），原文无对应，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What can we learn about the German spies in World War l, according to the talk?", "options": ["A) They were well paid by the German government.", "B) They gathered mostly in London during the war.", "C) They didn't provide any valuable information for Germany.", "D) They worked for both their own country and their enemies."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They worked for both their own country and their enemies.\n【定位】Spy expert Henry S. A. Becket writes that some German spies working in London during the\nwar also worked for the British. The British government had to make the Germans believe\ntheir spies were working. So, British officials gave them mostly false information. It was\ncalled chickenfeed.\n间谍专家亨利·S·A·贝克特写道，战争期间一些在伦敦活动的德国间谍同时也为英国人工作。\n英国政府必须让德国人相信他们的间谍仍在效力，于是英国官员主要给他们提供假情报，这\n种假情报就被称作\"鸡饲料\"。\n【信号】also worked for 揭双重身份。答案信号是 also worked for the British 里的 also（还/同\n时）：德国间谍\"在为德国之外，还为英国工作\"，一句点出\"双重效力\"这一关键事实，正是\n本题答案落点。\n【替换】some German spies working in London during the war also worked for the British ↔\nThey worked for both their own country and their enemies\n具体国名概括为\"本国与敌国\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nwell paid by the German government 盗用了录音中反复出现的 pay 和 government 等\n钱/政府词，但录音说的是 British government 给假情报，从未提德国政府给间谍\"高薪\"，\n听到 pay/government 就选会中招。\n借⽤原⽂：British officials gave them mostly false information\nB) 原词陷阱\ngathered mostly in London during the war 盗用了原文 working in London during the\nwar 的 London 与 during the war，但录音只说间谍在伦敦\"活动/工作\"，没说他们\"大多聚\n集在伦敦\"，把\"在伦敦工作\"偷换成\"大多聚集于伦敦\"。\n借⽤原⽂：some German spies working in London during the war\nC) 张冠李戴\ndidn't provide any valuable information for Germany 把\"英国给德国间谍假情报\"这件\n事，安成\"间谍没给德国有价值情报\"。原文是 British officials gave them mostly false\ninformation（英方给假料），主语和动作都被搬错；且\"any\"绝对化，原文未作此断言。\n借⽤原⽂：British officials gave them mostly false information", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What is the talk mainly about?", "options": ["A) Some common chicken food in America.", "B) The living and working conditions of Americans.", "C) The German and British spies in World War Two.", "D) Some expressions related to money."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） Some expressions related to money.\n【定位】I would like to talk about interesting words that have something to do with money today.\n今天我想谈谈一些和钱有关的有趣词语。\n【信号】开篇点题句。答案信号是开篇的点题句 interesting words that have something to do\nwith money：篇章独白常在第一段直接亮主题，此句已把全文圈定为\"与钱有关的词语\"，\n后文 chickenfeed、peanuts、peanut gallery 都是它的例子。\n【替换】interesting words that have something to do with money ↔ Some expressions related to money\nwords about money 概括为 expressions related to money\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nSome common chicken food in America 盗用 chickenfeed 这一原词，但鸡饲料只是用\n来解释 chickenfeed 这个习语来历的细节，仅覆盖第 2 段一处，撑不起全文主旨。\n借⽤原⽂：I am working for chickenfeed\nB) 过度推断\nThe living and working conditions of Americans 借了录音里 Americans、jobs 等词，\n但全文是借这些讲钱的习语，并非系统介绍美国人的生活与工作状况，覆盖面被无限放大。\n借⽤原⽂：Americans, like people in other countries, always want more money\nC) 原词陷阱\nThe German and British spies in World War Two 盗用第 4 段 German spies、British 等\n原词，但间谍故事只是 chickenfeed 一词的另一来历，仅占一段，且原文是 World War I\n而非选项写的 World War Two，既偏又错。\n借⽤原⽂：some German spies working in London during the war also worked for the British", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "Which of the following is the feature of a modern family?", "options": ["A) The husband is the head of the household.", "B) The husband usually makes big decisions.", "C) The wife lives within her husband's income.", "D) The wife works and does two kinds of jobs."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The wife works and does two kinds of jobs.\n【定位】In contrast, in many families today, the modern woman works outside the home even after\nshe has children. She's doing two jobs instead of one, so she is busier than the traditional\nmother was.\n与之相反，在今天的许多家庭里，现代女性即便有了孩子也仍在外工作。她做的是两份活而\n不是一份，所以比传统的母亲更忙。\n【信号】对比信号 In contrast + today。In contrast, in many families today, the modern\nwoman works outside the home 里 In contrast（与之相反）和 today、modern 一起\n出现，是\"现在和过去对着说\"的对比信号，紧跟其后的就是现代家庭的特征句，正是本题\n落点。\n【替换】the modern woman works outside the home ↔ The wife works\n同义替换 woman=wife\nShe's doing two jobs instead of one ↔ does two kinds of jobs\ntwo jobs = two kinds of jobs\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThe husband is the head of the household. 盗用了原文 The traditional husband was\nthe head of the household 的原词 head of the household，但那是\"传统\"丈夫的特征，\n本题问\"现代\"家庭，时代标签反了。\n借⽤原⽂：The traditional husband was the head of the household\nB) 与原文相反\nThe husband usually makes big decisions. 借用了原文 the traditional husband usually\nmade the big decisions about spending money，但原文说这是传统丈夫的做法，现代\n丈夫是 shares these decisions with his working wife，与\"现代家庭\"对不上。\n借⽤原⽂：the traditional husband usually made the big decisions about spending money\nC) 与原文相反\nThe wife lives within her husband's income. 几乎照搬原文 The traditional wife\nlearned to live within her husband's income，但那是传统妻子；现代妻子是 does not\nhave to live within her husband's income，意思正好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：The traditional wife learned to live within her husband's income", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Why were the school-age children of a traditional family more dependent?", "options": ["A) Their mother would help them to get ready for school and to make their breakfast.", "B) They had to help their mother and father to take care of their younger sisters and brothers.", "C) Their mother had to get up early in the morning to drive them to school.", "D) They were spoiled by their parents and didn't want to go to school."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\nTheir mother would help them to get ready for school and to make their breakfast.\n【定位】The school-age children of a traditional family were more dependent. Their mother was there\nto help them to get ready for school and to make their breakfast.\n传统家庭的学龄孩子更加依赖。他们的母亲在一旁帮他们准备上学、给他们做早餐。\n【信号】more dependent 后紧跟的解释句。The school-age children of a traditional family\nwere more dependent. 这句点出\"更依赖\"，紧接着的 Their mother was there to help\nthem to get ready for school and to make their breakfast 就是对\"为什么依赖\"的解释，\n是答案句。\n【替换】Their mother was there to help them to get ready for school and to make their breakfast ↔\nTheir mother would help them to get ready for school and to make their breakfast.\n近乎原句重述\n【排除】B) 未提及\nThey had to help their mother and father to take care of their younger sisters and\nbrothers. 说孩子帮忙照看弟弟妹妹，但原文从未提到\"younger sisters and brothers\"，是\n凭\"传统大家庭\"的常识臆造出来的。\nC) 张冠李戴\nTheir mother had to get up early in the morning to drive them to school. 里 get up\nearly in the morning、school 都是原文念过的词，但 get up early in the morning 说的\n是\"现代孩子\"，且原文是孩子\"自己\"早起、并没有 drive them to school（开车送）这件\n事。\n借⽤原⽂：They have to get up early in the morning and get ready for school\nD) 过度推断\nThey were spoiled by their parents and didn't want to go to school. 说孩子被宠坏、不\n想上学，原文谈的是\"依赖（dependent）\"而非\"被宠坏\"，也没说不想上学，是把\"依赖\"过\n度引申成了\"娇惯\"。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "Why is the American family today so different from that of fifty years ago?", "options": ["A) The roles of the father, mother, and children have changed.", "B) An increasing number of women work outside the home.", "C) Changes have taken place in the structure of an American family.", "D) The increasingly rapid pace of life makes people under greater pressure."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） An increasing number of women work outside the home.\n【定位】In the modern family, the roles of the father, mother, and children have changed as more and\nmore women work outside the home.\n在现代家庭中，随着越来越多的女性外出工作，父亲、母亲和孩子的角色都已发生变化。\n【信号】因果连词 as 引出原因。the roles of the father, mother, and children have changed as\nmore and more women work outside the home 里 as（随着/因为）连接前后：前半\n是\"角色变了\"这个现象，as 后面的 more and more women work outside the home 才\n是\"为什么变\"的原因，正是 Why 题落点。\n【替换】more and more women work outside the home ↔\nAn increasing number of women work outside the home.\nmore and more = an increasing number of\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nThe roles of the father, mother, and children have changed. 几乎照抄原文 the roles of\nthe father, mother, and children have changed，但这是 as 前面的\"现象\"——家庭哪里\n不同；题问的是\"为何不同\"，答的是原因，A 答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：the roles of the father, mother, and children have changed\nC) 答非所问\nChanges have taken place in the structure of an American family. 呼应原文 the\nAmerican family today is quite different 与结尾的 family structure，但它只是\"家庭结\n构发生了变化\"这个概括现象，同样没回答 Why。\n借⽤原⽂：The next century may bring more important changes to the American family structure\nD) 未提及\nThe increasingly rapid pace of life makes people under greater pressure. 说\"生活节奏\n加快带来压力\"，听上去像个原因，但原文通篇没提 pace of life 或 pressure，是凭常识自\n造的动因。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocates", "B) compact", "C) correlation", "D) exercise", "E) facilities", "F) interaction", "G) investigating", "H) overwhelmed", "I) particles", "J) permanent", "K) restricted", "L) simulating", "M) statistical", "N) tighten", "O) vulnerable"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) vulnerable\n形容词\n【词性槽】be + particularly + ___ + to 结构\n【依据】be vulnerable to 固定搭配 + 主旨呼应\nbe vulnerable to sth. 意为\"对……脆弱、易受……伤害\"——主语是 The developing\nbrain\"发育中的大脑\"，宾语是 the toxic effects of certain chemicals\"某些化学物质的毒\n性\"，整句意为\"发育中的大脑尤其易受这些毒性影响\"，正是全文的核心警告。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be + particularly +"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocates", "B) compact", "C) correlation", "D) exercise", "E) facilities", "F) interaction", "G) investigating", "H) overwhelmed", "I) particles", "J) permanent", "K) restricted", "L) simulating", "M) statistical", "N) tighten", "O) vulnerable"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) permanent\n形容词\n【词性槽】系动词 be 后表语\n【依据】呼应上文\"发育中大脑\"的不可逆损伤\nThe developing brain, the report says, is particularly __(26)__ to the toxic effects of\ncertain chemicals these products may contain, and the damage they cause can be\n__(27)__：前半句讲发育中的大脑特别脆弱，后半句用 and 顺承说\"造成的伤害可以是\n___\"——permanent\"永久的、不可逆的\"正好把\"伤害严重到什么程度\"推到极致，与开\n篇\"风险增加\"的警示基调一致。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— compact vs permanent 形容词同位。compact 在 can be\n后形容词位上形态完全合规，且\"短小\"听起来像在描述伤害规模——若不联系上文\"发育中\n的大脑易受影响\"很容易误选。防坑一句话：用 and 顺承同向延伸的判据，前半已经在讲\"风\n险增加\"，后半就该用 permanent\"不可逆\"把伤害推到极致。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·系动词 be 后表语"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocates", "B) compact", "C) correlation", "D) exercise", "E) facilities", "F) interaction", "G) investigating", "H) overwhelmed", "I) particles", "J) permanent", "K) restricted", "L) simulating", "M) statistical", "N) tighten", "O) vulnerable"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) advocates\n名词\n【词性槽】主语位、复数（have 提示）\n【依据】\"健康与环境倡导者\"= 推动政策的群体\nHealth and environmental advocates 是地道搭配，指\"健康与环境领域的倡导者/活动\n家\"——这群人正是\"敦促政府加紧管控\"的主体；与下文 called for more studies on their\nlong-term effects\"呼吁开展更多长期研究\"完全吻合。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语位、复数（have 提示）"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocates", "B) compact", "C) correlation", "D) exercise", "E) facilities", "F) interaction", "G) investigating", "H) overwhelmed", "I) particles", "J) permanent", "K) restricted", "L) simulating", "M) statistical", "N) tighten", "O) vulnerable"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) tighten\n动词\n【词性槽】to + 动词原形\n【依据】tighten the use\"收紧使用\"=加强管制\nurged US government agencies to tighten the use of some of the 11 chemicals：\ntighten 本义\"收紧\"，引申到法规语境就是\"加强管制\"，与下文 the Environmental\nProtection Agency __(30)__ the type and amount of lead\"EPA 限制了铅的类型和数\n量\"的具体举措一脉相承。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·to + 动词原形"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocates", "B) compact", "C) correlation", "D) exercise", "E) facilities", "F) interaction", "G) investigating", "H) overwhelmed", "I) particles", "J) permanent", "K) restricted", "L) simulating", "M) statistical", "N) tighten", "O) vulnerable"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) restricted\n动词\n【词性槽】句子谓语、需过去式\n【依据】\"限制了铅的类型与数量\"= 监管举措\nthe Environmental Protection Agency restricted the type and amount of lead that\ncould be present in paint and soil in homes and child-care facilities, after concerns\nwere raised about lead poisoning：EPA 因铅中毒担忧\"限制了\"铅的种类和数量——这是\n28 空 advocates、29 空 tighten 所推动的具体后续行动，链条完整。\n【竞争词】H) overwhelmed 搭配不通：无法\"压垮\"种类与数量\noverwhelm 的宾语通常是人或情感（如 overwhelmed by emotions），不能跟\"the type and\namount of lead\"这种抽象规格组合；而且语义\"EPA 压垮了铅的种类\"完全不合逻辑。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·句子谓语、需过去式"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocates", "B) compact", "C) correlation", "D) exercise", "E) facilities", "F) interaction", "G) investigating", "H) overwhelmed", "I) particles", "J) permanent", "K) restricted", "L) simulating", "M) statistical", "N) tighten", "O) vulnerable"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) facilities\n名词\n【词性槽】and 后并列名词、且需复数\n【依据】与 homes 并列指\"儿童看护场所\"\nlead that could be present in paint and soil in homes and child-care __(31)__：与\"家\n中\"并列的应该是另一个\"儿童会接触到油漆和土壤\"的物理场所；child-care facilities 是固\n定表达，意为\"儿童看护设施/托儿所\"，正好对位 homes。\n【竞争词】I) particles 语义不通：颗粒不是场所\nparticles 是\"微粒\"，child-care particles 既不是固定搭配，也无法与 homes（家这种场所）构\n成对位的并列项；本句讨论的是铅出现在\"哪些地方\"，需要的是场所名词，不是物质名词。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·and 后并列名词、且需复数"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocates", "B) compact", "C) correlation", "D) exercise", "E) facilities", "F) interaction", "G) investigating", "H) overwhelmed", "I) particles", "J) permanent", "K) restricted", "L) simulating", "M) statistical", "N) tighten", "O) vulnerable"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) investigating\n动词\n【词性槽】be + ___ + 名词宾语，需进行时分词\n【依据】\"调查毒性效应\"= 机构正在做的事\nThe agency is now investigating the toxic effects of some of the chemicals in the\nlatest report：机构\"正在调查\"最新报告中某些化学品的毒性，紧承上文 EPA 此前的限制铅\n举措、并推进到对新化学品的研究，正是政策\"仍在演进\"（The official policy, however, is\nstill evolving）的具体体现。\n【竞争词】L) simulating \"模拟毒性效应\"不合机构职能\nsimulate 是\"模拟、仿真\"，多用于实验/计算机建模；监管机构 EPA 的本职是调查、评估和管\n控，而不是\"模拟毒性效应\"。且语境是\"政策仍在演进\"，需要的是\"研究取证\"动作，不是\"实验\n模拟\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be + ___ + 名词宾语，需进行时"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocates", "B) compact", "C) correlation", "D) exercise", "E) facilities", "F) interaction", "G) investigating", "H) overwhelmed", "I) particles", "J) permanent", "K) restricted", "L) simulating", "M) statistical", "N) tighten", "O) vulnerable"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) statistical\n形容词\n【词性槽】形容词修饰名词 evidence\n【依据】EPA 监管需要\"统计学证据\"\n上文 Because children's brain and behavioral disorders, like hyperactivity and lower\ngrades, can also be linked to social and genetic factors：因为儿童脑部失常还可能由社\n会/遗传因素引起，所以要把它\"钉\"在特定化学品上必须有 statistical evidence\"统计学上的\n证据\"——即排除其它因素后的相关性数据，这是 EPA 监管的常规要求。\n【竞争词】J) permanent \"永久的证据\"不成立\npermanent 修饰的是状态/伤害（如 permanent damage），不修饰 evidence；\"永久证据\"既\n不是固定搭配也不合逻辑——证据要的是\"充分/确凿/统计学意义上的\"，不需要\"永久\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词修饰名词 evidence"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocates", "B) compact", "C) correlation", "D) exercise", "E) facilities", "F) interaction", "G) investigating", "H) overwhelmed", "I) particles", "J) permanent", "K) restricted", "L) simulating", "M) statistical", "N) tighten", "O) vulnerable"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) correlation\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 direct 后的名词\n【依据】与 but 后 associations 同义复现\nbut 后 noted strong associations between exposure and risk\"指出了暴露与风险之间的\n强相关\"——空处需要的是与 associations\"关联\"同义的词。correlation\"相关性\"正好与\nassociations 同义复现：哈佛研究\"没证明直接的相关性，但指出了强烈的关联\"，前后呼\n应、逻辑通顺。\n【竞争词】F) interaction \"互动\"不是研究证据的核心结论\ninteraction 是\"相互作用、互动\"，更多用于描述双方互相影响的过程；而本句讨论的是研究是\n否\"证明 A 导致 B\"的因果/相关性，需要的是 correlation/causation/link 这类关系词，不是\"互\n动\"。\"a direct interaction\"也不是流行病学/统计研究的标准结论表述。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— correlation vs interaction 易混。interaction 词性正确、\n且\"互动\"与本句\"暴露与风险的关系\"看上去也沾边——粗心考生很可能在不看 but 后的\nstrong associations 时误选 F。防坑一句话：but 后的 associations 就是空处的同义复\n现，研究证不证明的叫 correlation，不叫 interaction。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 direct 后的名词"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) advocates", "B) compact", "C) correlation", "D) exercise", "E) facilities", "F) interaction", "G) investigating", "H) overwhelmed", "I) particles", "J) permanent", "K) restricted", "L) simulating", "M) statistical", "N) tighten", "O) vulnerable"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) exercise\n动词\n【词性槽】to 后动词原形\n【依据】exercise caution = 保持谨慎（固定搭配）\nexercise 作动词时除了\"锻炼\"，还有\"行使、运用\"之意，exercise\ncaution/care/control/judgment 是英语中表示\"保持谨慎、运用判断力\"的高频搭配。\nWhile it may be impossible to prevent kids from drinking tap water that may\ncontain trace amounts of chemicals, keeping kids away from lawns recently sprayed\nwith chemicals and freshly dry-cleaned clothes can't hurt：后文给出\"远离喷药草坪、\n干洗衣物\"等具体小心举措，正是对\"保持谨慎\"的展开。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·to 后动词原形"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Not only moving objects and people but all systems have momentum.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】all systems have momentum\n【定位】we don't speak only of objects or people as having momentum; we speak of entire systems\nhaving momentum. Whether it's a sports team or a presidential campaign, everybody relishes\nhaving the big momentum\n我们不仅说物体或人有动量，也说整个系统有动量。无论是一支体育队还是一场总统竞选，\n每个人都希望握有大动量。\n【改写】Not only moving objects and people ↔\nwe don't speak only of objects or people as having momentum\nNot only…but 改写为 don't…only\nall systems have momentum ↔ we speak of entire systems having momentum\nall systems 与 entire systems 同义替换\n【辨邻】 B 该段讲的是 once he is moving, it is hard to change his state of motion，主语始终\n是物体或运动员，没有出现把动量主语扩展到 entire systems 的关键一步。\na heavy object, like a football player moving at a high speed, has a lot of momentum", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Changing the current energy system requires the systematic training of professionals and skilled labor.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】systematic training of professionals\n【定位】the engineers, designers, regulators, operators, and all of the other skilled people needed for\nthe new energy industry are specialists who have to be trained first…education, like any other\ncomplicated endeavor, takes time. And not only do our prospective new energy workers have\nto be trained, they have to be trained in the right sequence.\n新能源产业所需的工程师、设计师、监管者、操作工以及所有其他熟练人员都是必须先经过\n培训的专业人士……教育和其他复杂工程一样，需要时间。我们未来的新能源工人不仅要受\n训，还要按正确的次序受训。\n【改写】Changing the current energy system ↔ changes in our energy systems\n动名词改写\nsystematic training of professionals and skilled labor ↔ trained in the right sequence\nsystematic 改写为 in the right sequence\nprofessionals and skilled labor ↔\nengineers, designers, regulators, operators, and all of the other skilled people\n上位词对下位罗列", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Changing a light bulb is easier than changing the fixture housing it.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】灯泡 vs 灯具的难度对比\n【定位】It is easy to change a light bulb, sure, but it is harder to change the bulb and its fixture.\n换一只灯泡当然容易，但要把灯泡连同它的灯具一起换就更难了。\n【改写】Changing a light bulb is easier than changing the fixture housing it ↔\nit is easy to change a light bulb, sure, but it is harder to change the bulb and its fixture\n比较级改写为正反对照\n【辨邻】 D 该段说 it is quite easy, with all that choice, to change a light bulb，只完成了换灯\n泡容易这一半，没有进一步说换灯具更难，因此无法承载题干的对比。\nIt is quite easy, with all that choice, to change a light bulb", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Efforts to accelerate the current energy transitions didn't succeed as expected.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】加速能源转型未成功\n【定位】All the forecasts, plans, and anticipations cited above have failed so miserably because their\nauthors and promoters thought the transitions they hoped to implement would proceed unlike\nall previous energy transitions, and that their progress could be accelerated in an\nunprecedented manner.\n上文列举的所有预测、计划和期待之所以都惨遭失败，是因为它们的提出者和推动者以为，\n自己想推动的转型会有别于以往的所有能源转型，并且其进程可以被以前所未有的方式\n加速。\n【改写】Efforts to accelerate the current energy transitions ↔\ntheir progress could be accelerated in an unprecedented manner\nEfforts to accelerate 对位 could be accelerated\ndidn't succeed as expected ↔ have failed so miserably\n委婉否定换强烈否定\n【辨邻】 L 该段说的是 understanding energy system inertia and momentum can help you\ndecide whether their plans are feasible，是面向未来的判断建议，没有对已发生的\n加速努力下失败评判。\nWhen you hear people speaking of making a rapid transition toward any type of energy…\nunderstanding energy system inertia and momentum can help you decide whether their plans\nare feasible", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "To change the light source is costly because you have to change the whole fixture.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】换光源贵在要换整套灯具\n【定位】The whole fixture must be replaced—light source and luminaire—and this is never an\ninexpensive proposition.\n必须把整套灯具——光源加照明装置——一起换掉，这从来都不是一笔便宜的开销。\n【改写】To change the light source is costly ↔ this is never an inexpensive proposition\n双重否定换肯定\nyou have to change the whole fixture ↔ The whole fixture must be replaced\nhave to 与 must 同义\n【辨邻】 E 该段强调的是 harder to change the bulb and its fixture，重心在难度，没有 costly\n或 inexpensive 这类价格判断词，因此不能承担题干因 costly 才换整套这一因果\n论断。\nit is easy to change a light bulb, sure, but it is harder to change the bulb and its fixture", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Energy systems, like an aircraft carrier set in motion, have huge momentum.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】能源系统像航空母舰一样的动量\n【定位】our energy systems are a bit like an aircraft carrier: they are unbelievably expensive, they are\nbuilt to last for a very long time, they have a huge amount of inertia (meaning it takes a lot of\nenergy to set them moving), and they have a lot of momentum once they are set in motion.\n我们的能源系统有点像航空母舰：它们造价高得难以置信，被造来用很久，有巨大的惯性\n（意思是要花很多能量才能让它们动起来），一旦动起来又带着巨大的动量。\n【改写】Energy systems, like an aircraft carrier set in motion ↔\nour energy systems are a bit like an aircraft carrier\nlike 类比同源\nhave huge momentum ↔ they have a lot of momentum once they are set in motion\nhuge 与 a lot of 同义\n【辨邻】 B 该段说 Once you have started the boulder rolling, it develops momentum，主语\n是物理物体，不是能源系统，因此承担不了把能源系统比作大动量物体这一具体主张。\nonce he is moving, it is hard to change his state of motion", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The problem with lighting, if it arises, often doesn't lie in light sources but in their applications.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】问题不在光源而在应用\n【定位】As lighting expert Howard Brandston points out, \"Generally, there are no bad light sources,\nonly bad applications.\"\n正如照明专家霍华德·布兰德斯顿所说：通常没有不好的光源，只有不好的应用。\n【改写】often doesn't lie in light sources but in their applications ↔\nthere are no bad light sources, only bad applications\ndoesn't lie in X but in Y 与 no bad X, only bad Y 同义\n【辨邻】 F 该段强调 rooms and entire buildings have been designed in accordance with\nhow incandescent lighting reflects off walls and windows，方向是讲灯影响建\n筑，没有明说问题不在光源而在应用这一对立。\nrooms and entire buildings have been designed in accordance with how incandescent lighting\nreflects off walls and windows", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The biggest obstacle to energy transition is that the present energy system is too expensive to replace.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】现有能源系统太贵难替换\n【定位】By far the biggest type of momentum that comes into play when it comes to changing our\nenergy systems is economic momentum. The major components of our energy systems, such\nas fuel production, refining, electrical generation and distribution, are costly installations that\nhave lengthy life spans.\n在改变我们的能源系统时起作用的最大一类动量，是经济动量。我们能源系统的主要组成部\n分，比如燃料生产、精炼、发电与配电，都是耗资巨大、寿命极长的设施。\n【改写】The biggest obstacle to energy transition ↔\nBy far the biggest type of momentum that comes into play when it comes to changing our\nenergy systems\nobstacle 与 momentum 在本文同义\ntoo expensive to replace ↔ costly installations that have lengthy life spans\ntoo expensive 对位 costly + lengthy life spans\n【辨邻】 A 该段写 they are unbelievably expensive，是引入比喻级的整体描述，但没有把\nexpensive 这一项标为 the biggest 的最大阻力。\nthey are unbelievably expensive, they are built to last for a very long time", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "The application of a technology can impact areas beyond itself.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】技术影响波及自身之外\n【定位】One kind of momentum is technological momentum. When a technology is deployed, its\nimpacts reach far beyond itself.\n动量的一种是技术动量。一项技术一旦投入使用，其影响远远超出技术本身。\n【改写】The application of a technology ↔ When a technology is deployed\napplication 与 deployed 同义\ncan impact areas beyond itself ↔ its impacts reach far beyond itself\n动词与名词转换\n【辨邻】 E 该段展示 specialized light fixtures 这种技术影响的范围扩散，但没有把这一现象抽象\n为 a technology can impact areas beyond itself 这一普遍论断，论断在 D 段。\nAll of those specialized bulbs led to the building of specialized light fixtures\nF 该段把白炽灯影响推到 rooms and entire buildings，仍属于举例延伸，没有承担技\n术影响超出自身这一总论断。\nrooms and entire buildings have been designed in accordance with how incandescent lighting\nreflects off walls and windows", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Physical characteristics of moving objects help explain the dynamics of energy systems.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】用运动物体的物理特性解释能源系统\n【定位】In physics, moving objects have two characteristics relevant to understanding the dynamics of\nenergy systems: inertia and momentum.\n在物理学中，运动物体有两个对理解能源系统动力学很有用的特性：惯性与动量。\n【改写】Physical characteristics of moving objects ↔\nIn physics, moving objects have two characteristics\nPhysical 与 In physics 同义\nhelp explain the dynamics of energy systems ↔\nrelevant to understanding the dynamics of energy systems\nhelp explain 与 relevant to understanding 同义\n【辨邻】 A 该段用 our energy systems are a bit like an aircraft carrier 来类比，并未明说由\nmoving objects 的物理特性来解释 dynamics of energy systems。\nour energy systems are a bit like an aircraft carrier\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 用航空母舰作比，提出全文核心比喻：能源系统造价巨大、寿命极长、惯性与动量极\n强，政客承诺的快速能源转型并不现实。\n信息指纹 航空母舰比喻\nB 从物理学角度解释运动物体的两个关键特征——惯性（inertia）与动量\n（momentum），并用大圆石、橄榄球运动员作类比，为后文把能源系统比作大动量\n物体打底。\n信息指纹 物理学定义惯性与动量\nC\n把动量概念从物体、人扩展到整个系统：体育队、总统竞选这样的系统也有动量，谁\n掌握大动量就更难被阻止或转向。\n信息指纹 系统也有动量\nD 提出技术动量概念：一项技术一旦投入使用，其影响远超技术本身；以白炽灯为例，\n已发展出数百上千种形态，单换一只灯泡很容易。\n信息指纹 技术动量与白炽灯例\nE 白炽灯的动量并不止于灯泡本身：各种专用灯泡催生了书桌灯、天花板灯、烤箱灯等\n专用灯具，换灯泡容易，但要把灯泡和灯具一起换就更难了。\n信息指纹 灯泡易换灯具难换\nF 故事还没完：不仅灯具按白炽灯设计，连房间与整栋建筑都是按白炽光的反射效果设\n计的。\n信息指纹 建筑按白炽光设计\nG 引照明专家布兰德斯顿的话——没有不好的光源，只有不好的应用；并说明灯泡、灯\n具、房间三者必须协同，节能灯如果硬塞进白炽灯灯具，效果会很差，必须连光源和\n灯具一起换，而这从来都不便宜。\n信息指纹 光源与应用 + 整套替换昂贵\nH\n一句话补充布兰德斯顿的身份——他正是为自由女神像打灯的人，是一个权威背书的\n旁注。\n信息指纹 布兰德斯顿点亮自由女神像\nI 提出第二类动量——劳动力池动量：要让能源结构按比例切换，工程师、设计师、监\n管者、操作工等专业人才需要按正确次序成体系地培训，连高校培养方案都可能要\n改，难度叠加。\n信息指纹 劳动力池动量与系统培训\nJ\n最大的一类动量是经济动量：能源系统主要部件造价昂贵、寿命极长，核电站典型寿\n命四十到六十年，美国有煤电厂运转超过七十年，纽约哈德逊河上的水电站从 1898\n年商业运营至今。\n信息指纹 经济动量与超长寿命数字\nK 引斯米尔的话，断言此前对快速能源转型的预测、计划与期待都惨败，原因在于推动\n者一厢情愿地以为这次转型可以不同于以往、可以被前所未有地加速。\n信息指纹 斯米尔断言转型预测失败\nL\n全文收束：当听到有人谈论任何形式的快速能源转换时，理解能源系统的惯性与动量\n能帮助判断这些计划是否可行。\n信息指纹 用惯性动量判断方案可行性", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "Why did Jesse Jackson embrace the term \"African American\" for people of African descent?", "options": ["A) It is free from racial biases.", "B) It represents social progress.", "C) It is in the interest of common Americans.", "D) It follows the standard naming practice."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why did Jesse Jackson embrace the term \"African American\" 直接问某具体人物为何做\n某具体行为，原文必有原因句，属典型细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干为 Why did Jesse Jackson embrace the term，宾语后接同位语 \"African\nAmerican\" 解释 the term，再接介宾 for people of African descent 限定使用对象。结构\n平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】This one was chosen because it echoed the labels of groups, such as \"Italian Americans\" and\n\"Irish Americans,\" that had already been freed of widespread discrimination.\n选用这个称谓是因为它呼应了诸如“意大利裔美国人”和“爱尔兰裔美国人”等已经摆脱了广泛\n歧视的群体称谓。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nfreed of widespread discrimination ↔ free from racial biases\n预判要求答案表达“该称谓所呼应的标签已不带歧视”；free from racial biases 恰好把\nfreed of widespread discrimination 同义化，直接对应。\nB) 过度推断\nIt represents social progress 把“摆脱歧视”升级为“代表社会进步”，原文只说该称谓呼\n应的群体已不带歧视，并未把这个称谓本身定义为社会进步的象征。\nC) 偷换概念\nin the interest of common Americans 把“符合普通美国人的利益”塞给该称谓，但原文\nJesse Jackson declared that \"African American\" was the term to embrace 后给的\n理由只关乎被命名群体，与“普通美国人的利益”无关。\nD) 无中生有\nIt follows the standard naming practice 暗示存在某种“标准命名惯例”且本称谓遵循\n之，但原文只是说它呼应了若干已无歧视的群体标签，未提到任何“标准做法”。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author say about the naming of an ethnic group?", "options": ["A) It advances with the times.", "B) It is based on racial roots.", "C) It merits intensive study.", "D) It is politically sensitive."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the naming of an ethnic group 直接问作者就某话题\n给出的看法，答案在原文有明确表述，属作者观点类细节题。\n【拆句】句子结构是 What does the author say about the naming of an ethnic group，介词\nabout 引出谈论对象，of an ethnic group 是 naming 的后置修饰。结构清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】A century's worth of calculated name changes points to the fact that naming any group is a\npolitically freighted exercise.\n一个世纪以来精心计算过的名称变更，指向这样一个事实：给任何群体命名都是一项在政治\n上分量沉重的行为。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIt advances with the times 说命名“随时代进步”，原文只说名称随时代发生 calculated\nname changes——是“变更”，不是“进步”，作者并未对每一次变更给出正面评价。\nB) 无中生有\nIt is based on racial roots 把命名归因到“种族根源”，但原文未提任何“种族根源”作为命\n名的依据，作者强调的是政治权衡。\nC) 无中生有\nIt merits intensive study 把作者观点偷换成“值得深入研究”，原文仅在第 2 段末提及 A\n2001 study cataloged，那是已有研究的引证，而非作者主张命名“值得被研究”。\nD) ✓ 正确\npolitically freighted exercise ↔ politically sensitive\n预判要求答案聚焦“政治分量重”；只有 It is politically sensitive 保留了 politically 这一\n关键修饰，其他选项均改换或放弃了政治维度。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do Erika Hall's findings indicate?", "options": ["A) Racial biases are widespread in the professional world.", "B) Many applicants don't attend to details on their resumes.", "C) Job seekers should all be careful about their affiliations.", "D) Most recruiters are unable to control their racial biases."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do Erika Hall's findings indicate 问的是研究“表明”了什么，原文中有对应陈述句，\n仍属细节题，而非需要二步推理的推断题。\n【拆句】题干主干为 What do Erika Hall's findings indicate；findings 是复数主语，所以用 do。\n结构简单、无陷阱。\n【定位】The study's most striking findings shed light on the racial biases permeating the\nprofessional world.\n该研究最引人注目的发现，揭示了弥漫于职业界的种族偏见。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe racial biases permeating the professional world ↔\nRacial biases are widespread in the professional world\n预判要求答案表达“研究发现揭示了职场普遍存在种族偏见”；Racial biases are\nwidespread in the professional world 把 racial biases permeating the professional\nworld 翻成主系表，与定位句信息完全一致。\nB) 张冠李戴\nMany applicants don't attend to details on their resumes 说求职者没注意简历细\n节，原文实际说的是 Even seemingly harmless details on a resume, it appears, can\ntap into recruiters' biases——主语应是招聘者的偏见被激活，并非求职者疏忽。\nC) 过度推断\nJob seekers should all be careful about their affiliations 是给求职者的建议，原文并\n未对求职者发出 should 类建议；且 all 一词把范围扩到所有人，过度推断。\nD) 过度推断\nMost recruiters are unable to control their racial biases 给招聘者贴上“无法控制”的\n标签，原文只说招聘者会被简历细节“激活”偏见 tap into recruiters' biases，并未说他\n们“不可控制”自己的偏见。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does Erika Hall find in her experiment about a man with the last name Williams?", "options": ["A) African Americans fare better than many other ethnic groups.", "B) Black people's socioeconomic status in America remains low.", "C) People's conception of a person has much to do with the way he or she is labeled.", "D) One's professional standing and income are related to their educational background."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does Erika Hall find in her experiment 问研究者从该实验中得出的结论，原文有总\n结句，仍是细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does Erika Hall find in her experiment，后置 about 短语 about a man\nwith the last name Williams 限定实验对象；with 引出后置修饰说明姓氏是 Williams。结\n构清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】To one group, he was identified as \"African-American,\" and another was told he was \"Black.\"\nWith little else to go on, they were asked to estimate Mr. Williams's salary, professional\nstanding, and educational background.\n对一组被试，他被称作“非裔美国人”；对另一组，他被说成“黑人”。在几乎没有其他信息可\n依据的情况下，他们被要求估算 Williams 先生的薪水、职业地位与教育背景。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nAfrican Americans fare better than many other ethnic groups 把比较对象设为“非\n裔美国人 vs 其他族裔群体”，但实验中比较的是同一个 Williams 先生被贴上不同标签时\n的评估差异，根本不是族裔间的对照。\nB) 偷换概念\nBlack people's socioeconomic status in America remains low 把估值结果当成“黑人\n现实社会经济地位低”的事实陈述，但实验测的是被试的“估值/认知”，并非真实社会经济\n地位。\nC) ✓ 正确\nhe was identified as \"African-American,\" and another was told he was \"Black\" ↔\nthe way he or she is labeled\nput his salary at about $29,000, and guessed that he had only \"some\" college experience ↔\nPeople's conception of a person\n预判要求答案表达“称谓不同→评估不同”；People's conception of a person has\nmuch to do with the way he or she is labeled 用 has much to do with（与…紧密相\n关）把因果关系等值表达——是唯一保留实验结论方向的选项。\nD) 无中生有\nOne's professional standing and income are related to their educational\nbackground 把话题拉到“职位收入与学历相关”，但原文实验仅用学历、收入、职级三个\n维度刻画被试“对同一个人的不同认知”，并未论证三者之间的因果。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is Dr. Du Bois's ideal?", "options": ["A) All Americans enjoy equal rights.", "B) A person is judged by their worth.", "C) A new term is created to address African Americans.", "D) All ethnic groups share the nation's continued progress."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is Dr. Du Bois's ideal 直问 Du Bois 的理想是什么，原文有引语直接给出，是典型细\n节题。\n【拆句】题干为 What is Dr. Du Bois's ideal，所属格 Dr. Du Bois's 修饰 ideal，整体只有主系表三\n部分。结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】Perhaps a new phrase is needed, one that can bring everyone one big step closer to realizing\nDu Bois's original, idealistic hope: \"It's not the name—it's the Thing that counts.\"\n或许需要一个新短语，使每个人都向 Du Bois 最初那个理想化的希望——“重要的不是名\n字，而是事物本身”——迈进一大步。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nAll Americans enjoy equal rights 谈“所有美国人享有平等权利”，但 Du Bois 的引语只\n对照“名字 vs 事物本身”，未涉及“权利平等”这一维度。\nB) ✓ 正确\nit's the Thing that counts ↔ A person is judged by their worth\n预判要求答案表达“评判看本质而非名称”；A person is judged by their worth 把 it's\nthe Thing that counts 同义化为“按内在价值评判一个人”，方向与引语一致。\nC) 张冠李戴\nA new term is created to address African Americans 实际上是该段作者本人提出的\n设想 Perhaps a new phrase is needed，并非 Du Bois 的理想——Du Bois 的理想是\n引号内的内容。\nD) 偷换概念\nAll ethnic groups share the nation's continued progress 把对象抬升到“所有族群共\n享国家进步”，但引语关心的是评判个体（或事物）的依据，而非族群共享进步。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is Arum and Roksa's finding about higher education in America?", "options": ["A) It aims at stimulating the intellectual curiosity of college students.", "B) It fails to prepare students to face the challenges of modern times.", "C) It has experienced dramatic changes in recent years.", "D) It has tried hard to satisfy students' various needs."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is Arum and Roksa's finding about higher education in America? 直接问两位研\n究者关于美国高等教育的发现，属于可以回原文找客观陈述的细节题。\n【拆句】题干主干 What is Arum and Roksa's finding，about 引出的介词短语 about higher\neducation in America 限定 finding 的范围；结构简单、无陷阱。\n【定位】In particular, Arum and Roksa found, college students were not developing the critical\nthinking, analytic reasoning and other higher-level skills that are necessary to thrive in today's\nknowledge-based economy and to lead our nation in a time of complex challenges and\ndynamic change.\n尤其值得注意的是，Arum 和 Roksa 发现，大学生没有培养出在当今知识经济中立足、在复\n杂多变的时代领导国家所必需的批判性思维、分析推理和其他更高阶的能力。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt aims at stimulating the intellectual curiosity of college students 把高校说成\"志\n在激发求知欲\"，与原文 the lack of intellectual growth experienced by many\npeople enrolled in college 的批评判断完全相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\nwere not developing the critical thinking, analytic reasoning and other higher-level skills that are\nnecessary to thrive in today's knowledge-based economy\n↔ fails to prepare students to face the challenges of modern times\n预判的\"大学生没培养出应对当今社会的高阶能力\"恰好与 fails to prepare students to\nface the challenges of modern times 一一对应，且是研究的 finding，故选 B。\nC) 无中生有\nIt has experienced dramatic changes in recent years 称美国高等教育近年发生剧\n变，但原文只批判它培养不出高阶能力，从未谈论\"剧变\"。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt has tried hard to satisfy students' various needs 称高校\"竭力满足学生需求\"，与\n原文 not doing a very good job 的负面判断相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What is responsible for the students' lack of higher-level skills?", "options": ["A) The diluted college curriculum.", "B) The boring classroom activities.", "C) The absence of rigorous discipline.", "D) The outdated educational approach."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is responsible for the students' lack of higher-level skills? 问的是一个明确的原\n因，回原文能找到对应的归因句。\n【拆句】What is responsible for the students' lack of higher-level skills? 主干是 What is\nresponsible for X，X 是 the students' lack of higher-level skills；注意 lack of 是含蓄否\n定，问的是\"造成缺乏\"的原因，不要读反。\n【定位】Arum and Roksa placed the blame for students' lack of learning on a watered-down college\ncurriculum and lowered undergraduate work standards.\nArum 和 Roksa 把学生学习不足的原因归咎于被注水的大学课程和被降低的本科学习标准。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na watered-down college curriculum ↔ The diluted college curriculum\n定位句给出的两项原因之一就是 watered-down college curriculum，A 项 The\ndiluted college curriculum 与之等值，是唯一命中原文归因的选项。\nB) 无中生有\nThe boring classroom activities 中 boring 在原文无任何依据；原文谈的是课程被注\n水、学习时间太少，而不是课堂活动本身\"无聊\"。\nC) 偷换概念\nThe absence of rigorous discipline 中 discipline 一般指\"纪律/管教\"，原文讲的是\nlowered undergraduate work standards（学术要求降低），\"学术要求\"与\"纪律\"不是\n同一概念。\nD) 无中生有\nThe outdated educational approach 谈\"过时的教学方法\"，原文从未涉及方法\"新\n旧\"问题，仅指课程被稀释、要求降低。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does Laura Hamilton say about college administrations?", "options": ["A) They fail to give adequate help to the needy students.", "B) They tend to offer too many less challenging courses.", "C) They seem to be out of touch with society.", "D) They prioritize non-academic activities."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does Laura Hamilton say about college administrations? 问 Hamilton 对\ncollege administrations 的具体说法，是有据可查的细节题。\n【拆句】What does Laura Hamilton say about college administrations? 一般疑问倒装结构，主\n干是 Laura Hamilton say what about college administrations，结构无难度。\n【定位】Laura Hamilton, the author of a study on parents who pay for college, will argue in a\nforthcoming book that college administrations are overly concerned with the social and\nathletic activities of their students.\nLaura Hamilton——一项关于\"为孩子付学费的家长\"研究的作者——将在一本即将出版的\n书中论证：高校管理层过度关心学生的社交和运动活动。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThey fail to give adequate help to the needy students 说\"高校未能帮助贫困学生\"，\n原文谈的是\"迎合富裕学生\"而非\"未帮助贫困生\"，且\"未能给予帮助\"不是 Hamilton 的直\n接观点。\nB) 偷换概念\nThey tend to offer too many less challenging courses 把\"开太多容易课\"算到高校管\n理层头上，但原文只说\"被各种俱乐部+一批更轻松的专业辅助\"，描述的是路径现象，并\n未把开课主体写成 administrations 主动\"提供\"过多容易课。\nC) 无中生有\nThey seem to be out of touch with society 说\"高校与社会脱节\"，原文 Hamilton 段\n对此没有任何陈述。\nD) ✓ 正确\noverly concerned with the social and athletic activities of their students ↔\nThey prioritize non-academic activities\n预判的\"过度关注社交和运动\"恰好被 D 项以\"优先考虑非学业活动\"概括，与 Hamilton\n的观点完全等值。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What can be learned about the socially and financially privileged students?", "options": ["A) They tend to have a sense of superiority over their peers.", "B) They can afford to choose easier majors in order to enjoy themselves.", "C) They spend a lot of time building strong connections with businesses.", "D) They can climb the social ladder even without a degree."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What can be learned about the socially and financially privileged students? 直接\n问\"关于这类学生可以了解到什么\"，是明示信息的细节题。\n【拆句】What can be learned about the socially and financially privileged students? 被动语态\ncan be learned about X，X 由 socially and financially 两个副词共同修饰 privileged 形\n成名词短语，理解上无难点。\n【定位】By sanctioning this watered-down version of college, universities are \"catering to the social\nand educational needs of wealthy students at the expense of others\" who won't enjoy the\nfinancial backing or social connections of richer students once they graduate.\n通过纵容这种被注水的大学模式，大学\"在迎合富裕学生的社交和教育需求时牺牲了其他\n人\"——这些其他人毕业后将不享有富裕学生的财力支持或社会关系。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nThey tend to have a sense of superiority over their peers 谈\"优越感\"，原文虽暗示\n这类学生条件更好，但从未谈\"心理上的优越感\"或对同侪的态度。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe \"party pathway,\" which eases many students through college, helped along by various clubs\nthat send students into the party scene and a host of easier majors\n↔ They can afford to choose easier majors in order to enjoy themselves\ncatering to the social and educational needs of wealthy students ↔\nThey can afford to choose easier majors in order to enjoy themselves\n预判中的\"被注水路径+轻松专业\"在 B 项里被合成为\"有条件选轻松专业以享乐\"，且经济\n底气来自原文 wealthy students 的描述，三点对齐，故选 B。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey spend a lot of time building strong connections with businesses 说他们\"花\n大量时间与企业建立联系\"，原文 social connections of richer students 描述的是他们\n毕业时已经拥有的家庭/社会人脉，并不是\"在校期间主动与商界建连接\"。\nD) 过度推断\nThey can climb the social ladder even without a degree 说\"无文凭也能上升\"，原文\nThese students need to build skills and knowledge during college if they are to\nuse their degrees as a stepping-stone to middle-class mobility 在讨论\"靠文凭往上\n走\"的对象是非富裕生，且并未说富裕生不需要文凭。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author suggest in the last paragraph?", "options": ["A) American higher education has lost its global competitiveness.", "B) People should not expect too much from American higher education.", "C) The current situation in American higher education may not last long.", "D) It will take a long time to change the current trend in higher education."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest in the last paragraph? 含 suggest 推断信号，又指定末\n段范围，是末段范围内的小推断题。\n【拆句】What does the author suggest in the last paragraph? 主干 the author suggest\nwhat，介词短语 in the last paragraph 限定推断范围；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】For students who have been coasting through college, and for American universities that have\nbeen demanding less work, offering more attractions and charging higher tuition, the party\nmay soon be over.\n对于一直在大学里得过且过的学生，以及一直在降低要求、增加诱惑、提高学费的美国大学\n来说，这场派对很快就要结束了。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nAmerican higher education has lost its global competitiveness 说美国高教\"已经失\n去全球竞争力\"，原文只说全球经济不会原谅劳动力能力下滑，未做\"已失去竞争力\"的现\n状判定。\nB) 偷换概念\nPeople should not expect too much from American higher education 把作者写\n成\"劝读者降低期望\"，但作者通篇是批评高校、敦促其改变，而不是劝读者放弃期望。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe party may soon be over ↔\nThe current situation in American higher education may not last long\n预判的\"现状不会持续\"与 The current situation in American higher education may\nnot last long 一一对应，且未越过原文一步，符合 suggest 推断的边界。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt will take a long time to change the current trend in higher education 说\"改变现\n状要花很长时间\"，原文 the party may soon be over 明确暗示改变将很快到来，方向\n完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}]});
